var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_fetch_and_supply/#Fetch-and-supply-limitation","page":"Fetch and supply limitation","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nFetch with unlimited supply\nSupply limitation\nReferences","title":"Fetch and supply limitation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_fetch_and_supply/#Overview","page":"Fetch and supply limitation","text":"AeoLiS solves an advection equation for the sediment concentration in the air C, which relaxes towards the saturated concentration Cu over the adaptation time T (Hoonhout and de Vries 2016):\n\nfracpartial Cpartial t + u fracpartial Cpartial x = fracC_u - CT\n\nIn steady state with unlimited supply, the concentration grows along the fetch as the solution of an ordinary differential equation, dC/dx = (Cu - C) / (u T). This vignette checks the model against that solution, computed with OrdinaryDiffEq, and then limits the supply with a non-erodible layer.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing OrdinaryDiffEq\nusing Plots","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_fetch_and_supply/#Fetch-with-unlimited-supply","page":"Fetch and supply limitation","text":"A flat transect of 60 m with grain velocities equal to the wind (method_grainspeed = :windspeed) makes u and Cu uniform. The adaptation time is raised to 0.2 s so the adaptation length u T spans a few cells.\n\nx = collect(0.0:0.5:60.0)\np = AeolisParameters(; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=fill(2.0, length(x)),\n                     wind_file=[0.0 10.0 270.0; 86400.0 10.0 270.0], grain_size=0.0003,\n                     method_grainspeed=:windspeed, T=0.2, dt=60.0, tstop=600.0,\n                     output_times=600.0, output_vars=[:Ct, :Cu, :u])\nsol = solve(AeolisProblem(p))\nC = transect(sol, :Ct)[:, 1, end]\nCu = transect(sol, :Cu)[2, 1, end]\nu = transect(sol, :u)[2, 1, end]\n(Cu=Cu, u=u, adaptation_length=u * p.T)\n\n(Cu = 0.00023007629540473118, u = 10.0, adaptation_length = 2.0)\n\nThe fetch equation, solved with an adaptive ODE solver:\n\nfetch(C, par, x) = (par.Cu - C) / (par.u * par.T)\node = ODEProblem(fetch, 0.0, (0.0, 60.0), (; Cu, u, T=p.T))\nexact = solve(ode, Tsit5(); reltol=1e-10, abstol=1e-12)\nscatter(x, C; ms=2, label=\"Aeolis (implicit upwind, dx = 0.5 m)\", xlims=(0, 30),\n        xlabel=\"fetch (m)\", ylabel=\"concentration (kg/m²)\")\nplot!(exact; lw=2, label=\"fetch ODE (Tsit5)\")\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe implicit first-order upwind scheme of AeoLiS gives the exponential approach with a numerical diffusion that depends on the cell size dx compared with u T. For each cell the discrete solution is C_i = (C_{i-1} + a Cu) / (1 + a) with a = dx / (u T), so it lags the exact solution; the gap shrinks as dx / (u T) falls:\n\nerr(dx) = begin\n    xx = collect(0.0:dx:60.0)\n    q = remake_parameters(p; xgrid_file=xx, bed_file=fill(2.0, length(xx)))\n    s = solve(AeolisProblem(q))\n    c = transect(s, :Ct)[:, 1, end]\n    maximum(abs.(c .- exact.(xx))) / Cu\nend\n[(dx=dx, max_relative_error=round(err(dx); sigdigits=3)) for dx in (2.0, 1.0, 0.5, 0.25)]\n\n4-element Vector{@NamedTuple{dx::Float64, max_relative_error::Float64}}:\n (dx = 2.0, max_relative_error = 0.132)\n (dx = 1.0, max_relative_error = 0.0766)\n (dx = 0.5, max_relative_error = 0.0417)\n (dx = 0.25, max_relative_error = 0.0219)","title":"Fetch with unlimited supply"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_fetch_and_supply/#Supply-limitation","page":"Fetch and supply limitation","text":"Now the bed carries only 2 mm of loose sand over a non-erodible layer (ne_file, with th_nelayer making the threshold infinite where the bed reaches it). The upwind part of the transect is stripped of sand, after which the fetch starts further downwind.\n\nx = collect(0.0:1.0:100.0)\nq = AeolisParameters(; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=fill(2.0, length(x)),\n                     ne_file=fill(2.0 - 0.012, length(x)), th_nelayer=true,\n                     wind_file=[0.0 12.0 270.0; 86400.0 12.0 270.0], grain_size=0.0003,\n                     nlayers=3, layer_thickness=0.002, dt=600.0, tstop=12 * 3600.0,\n                     output_times=3600.0, output_vars=[:zb, :Ct, :qs])\nsl = solve(AeolisProblem(q))\nzb = transect(sl, :zb)\nplot(x, 1000 .* (zb[:, 1:3:end] .- zb[:, 1]); xlabel=\"x (m)\",\n     ylabel=\"bed level change (mm)\", label=permutedims([\"t = $(Int(t / 3600)) h\"\n                                                        for t in sl.t[1:3:end]]),\n     xlims=(0, 60))\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe flux leaving the transect falls as the sand runs out:\n\nqs = transect(sl, :qs)\nplot(sl.t ./ 3600, qs[end - 1, 1, :]; lw=2, marker=:circle, xlabel=\"time (h)\",\n     ylabel=\"flux at x = 99 m (kg/m/s)\", label=\"\")\n\n(Image: )","title":"Supply limitation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/04_fetch_and_supply/#References","page":"Fetch and supply limitation","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Hoonhout2016\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nHoonhout, Bas M., and Sierd de Vries. 2016. “A Process-Based Model for Aeolian Sediment Transport and Spatiotemporal Varying Sediment Availability.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface 121: 1555–75. https://doi.org/10.1002/2015JF003692.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/10_schemes_and_solvers/#Numerical-schemes-and-solvers","page":"Numerical schemes and solvers","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nA test problem\nSchemes and solvers\nLinear solvers\nReferences","title":"Numerical schemes and solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/10_schemes_and_solvers/#Overview","page":"Numerical schemes and solvers","text":"The concentration in the air is advanced by a numerical scheme, chosen as the algorithm passed to solve: implicit Euler (EulerBackward, the AeoLiS default), CrankNicolson, or explicit Euler (EulerForward, with the time step limited by the CFL number). Inside a scheme the transport solver is Trunk (an upwind discretisation solved as a sparse linear system), Pieter (a finite-volume form with fluxes on cell faces) or SteadyState (an upwind sweep that finds the equilibrium with the current wind). The sparse systems are solved with LinearSolve.jl, so any of its sparse factorizations can be used.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing LinearSolve\nusing Plots","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/10_schemes_and_solvers/#A-test-problem","page":"Numerical schemes and solvers","text":"A beach with a varying wind, so that the concentration is not always in equilibrium:\n\nx = collect(0.0:1.0:100.0)\nt = 0.0:600.0:86400.0\nwind = hcat(t, 8 .+ 6 .* sin.(t ./ 3000.0) .^ 2, fill(270.0, length(t)))\np = AeolisParameters(; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=fill(1.0, length(x)), wind_file=wind,\n                     grain_size=0.0003, dt=300.0, tstop=6 * 3600.0, output_times=1800.0,\n                     output_vars=[:zb, :qs], CFL=1.0)\nprob = AeolisProblem(p)\n\nAeolisProblem with tspan (0.0, 21600.0)\nAeolisParameters\n  time: 0.0 to 21600.0 s, dt = 300.0 s\n  scheme: euler_backward, solver: trunk\n  fractions: 1, layers: 3\n  enabled: process_wind, process_transport, process_bedupdate, process_threshold, th_grainsize","title":"A test problem"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/10_schemes_and_solvers/#Schemes-and-solvers","page":"Numerical schemes and solvers","text":"algs = [\"EulerBackward(Trunk())\" => EulerBackward(Trunk()),\n        \"CrankNicolson(Trunk())\" => CrankNicolson(Trunk()),\n        \"EulerForward(Trunk())\" => EulerForward(Trunk()),\n        \"EulerBackward(Pieter())\" => EulerBackward(Pieter()),\n        \"EulerBackward(SteadyState())\" => EulerBackward(SteadyState())]\nsols = Dict(name => solve(prob, alg) for (name, alg) in algs)\nplt = plot(; xlabel=\"time (h)\", ylabel=\"flux at x = 99 m (kg/m/s)\", legend=:topleft)\nfor (name, _) in algs\n    s = sols[name]\n    plot!(plt, s.t ./ 3600, transect(s, :qs)[end - 1, 1, :]; lw=2, label=name)\nend\nplt\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe explicit scheme takes many more steps, since the CFL number limits its time step to the time the wind takes to cross a cell; the solution statistics count the time steps and the matrix solves:\n\n[(name, get(sols[name].stats, :time, 0), get(sols[name].stats, :matrixsolve, 0))\n for (name, _) in algs]\n\n5-element Vector{Tuple{String, Int64, Int64}}:\n (\"EulerBackward(Trunk())\", 73, 73)\n (\"CrankNicolson(Trunk())\", 73, 73)\n (\"EulerForward(Trunk())\", 232179, 0)\n (\"EulerBackward(Pieter())\", 73, 72)\n (\"EulerBackward(SteadyState())\", 73, 0)\n\nThe bed level after six hours differs by less than a millimetre between the time-dependent schemes; the steady-state solver, which ignores the time the concentration takes to adjust, differs by a few millimetres:\n\nref = transect(sols[\"EulerBackward(Trunk())\"], :zb)[:, end]\n[(name, maximum(abs.(transect(sols[name], :zb)[:, end] .- ref))) for (name, _) in algs]\n\n5-element Vector{Tuple{String, Float64}}:\n (\"EulerBackward(Trunk())\", 0.0)\n (\"CrankNicolson(Trunk())\", 1.3388266431046958e-6)\n (\"EulerForward(Trunk())\", 0.0006005299166618183)\n (\"EulerBackward(Pieter())\", 1.2569002748152869e-5)\n (\"EulerBackward(SteadyState())\", 0.005272247071415048)","title":"Schemes and solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/10_schemes_and_solvers/#Linear-solvers","page":"Numerical schemes and solvers","text":"The trunk and pieter solvers assemble one sparse matrix per fraction and time step and solve it repeatedly while the fraction weights are adjusted to the available sediment; the factorization is reused across these solves. A LinearSolve.jl algorithm can be given with linsolve:\n\nfor lin in (nothing, KLUFactorization(), UMFPACKFactorization())\n    s = solve(prob, EulerBackward(Trunk(); linsolve=lin))\n    d = maximum(abs.(transect(s, :zb)[:, end] .- ref))\n    println(rpad(string(something(lin, \"default\")), 30), \" max difference \", d)\nend\n\ndefault                        max difference 0.0\nLinearSolve.KLUFactorization(true, true) max difference 0.0\nLinearSolve.UMFPACKFactorization{@NamedTuple{}}(true, true, NamedTuple()) max difference 1.1102230246251565e-16","title":"Linear solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/10_schemes_and_solvers/#References","page":"Numerical schemes and solvers","text":"","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/13_marram_grass/#Marram-grass-in-one-cell","page":"Marram grass in one cell","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nTraits and evidence\nThe process functions\nCalibration experiments\nBurial in a mesocosm\nBurial in the field\nDrought, flooding and heat\nTen years of a stand\nWhat is not yet known","title":"Marram grass in one cell"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/13_marram_grass/#Overview","page":"Marram grass in one cell","text":"Marram grass (Calamagrostis arenaria, formerly Ammophila arenaria) builds the foredunes of north-west Europe. It grows fastest when it is buried by about 0.3 m of sand per growing season (Nolet et al. 2018). It declines where sand stops arriving, because root-feeding nematodes and pathogens build up in the sand around its roots (van der Putten et al. 1993; van der Stoel et al. 2002).\n\nAeolis.jl has a model of marram grass in one grid cell. Each process is a small function of the traits, with its source and evidence grade in its docstring. This vignette:\n\nshows the process functions;\nre-runs the published experiments to which the traits were calibrated;\nfollows a stand for ten years as sand supply changes.\n\nThe next vignette puts the model on a grid.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing Dates\nusing Plots\nusing Printf\nconst A = Aeolis\ntr = MarramTraits()","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/13_marram_grass/#Traits-and-evidence","page":"Marram grass in one cell","text":"The traits are listed by marram_info. Each has a unit, a range, a source, and an evidence grade:\n\nM: measured on marram;\nD: derived from measurements, here mostly by the calibration below;\nP: a prior without a direct measurement.\n\ninfo = marram_info()\nfor g in (:M, :D, :P)\n    println(g, \": \", count(r -> r.evidence == g, info), \" traits\")\nend\nfor r in info[[4, 5, 6, 7, 8]]\n    @printf(\"%-12s %6.3g %-12s %s  %s\\n\", r.name, r.default, r.unit, r.evidence, r.source)\nend\n\nM: 31 traits\nD: 16 traits\nP: 18 traits\nburial_window     30 day          P  Nolet2018\nburial_opt     0.31 m/season     M  Nolet2018\nburial_c_lo       2 m/season     D  Reijers2021\nburial_c_hi    0.26 m/season     M  Nolet2018\nr               6.3 1/year       D  Reijers2021","title":"Traits and evidence"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/13_marram_grass/#The-process-functions","page":"Marram grass in one cell","text":"Four of the processes, as functions of their driver:\n\nBurial: growth responds to the rate of burial in m per growing season, the unit used by Nolet et al. (2018). Above the optimum it falls with Nolet’s width.\nSoil-biota pressure: without burial, pressure builds up slowly at first. It costs a quarter of the vigour after three years, and it is why the field response of Nolet et al. (2018) falls below the optimum. Vigour lowers both the growth rate and the shoot density the roots can sustain.\nWater: stress acts through the root zone, with the thresholds of Homberger et al. (2026).\nFlooding: survival falls with days of continuous submergence in seawater (Aptekar and Rejmánek 2000).\n\nd = 0:0.01:1.0\np1 = plot(d, [A.burial_response(x, tr) for x in d]; lw=2, legend=false,\n          xlabel=\"burial (m per season)\", ylabel=\"G_b\", title=\"burial\")\nyrs = 0:0.05:6\np2 = plot(yrs, [A.vigour(A.pressure_step(0.0, 0.0, y * 365.25, tr), tr) for y in yrs];\n          lw=2, legend=false, xlabel=\"years without burial\", ylabel=\"V\",\n          title=\"soil-biota pressure\")\nθ = 0:0.001:0.08\np3 = plot(100θ, [A.water_stress(x / tr.porosity, tr) for x in θ]; lw=2, legend=false,\n          xlabel=\"root-zone moisture (% by volume)\", ylabel=\"f_W\", title=\"water\")\nD = 0:0.1:20\np4 = plot(D, [A.flood_survival(x, tr) for x in D]; lw=2, legend=false,\n          xlabel=\"days submerged\", ylabel=\"survival\", title=\"flooding\")\nplot(p1, p2, p3, p4; layout=(2, 2), size=(800, 600))\n\n(Image: )","title":"The process functions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/13_marram_grass/#Calibration-experiments","page":"Marram grass in one cell","text":"marram_experiment re-runs each experiment and returns the model’s numbers next to the published ones.","title":"Calibration experiments"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/13_marram_grass/#Burial-in-a-mesocosm","page":"Marram grass in one cell","text":"Reijers et al. (2021) buried tussocks in 6.25 m² mesocosms of fresh sand by 0, 2 or 4 cm every fortnight for a year. That is 0, 0.26 or 0.52 m per growing season.\n\ne = marram_experiment(:reijers2021)\nfor i in 1:3\n    @printf(\"%.2f m/season: shoots %5.0f (observed %3.0f), root:shoot %.2f (observed %.1f)\\n\",\n            e.burial[i], e.shoots[i], e.shoots_observed[i], e.ratio[i], e.ratio_observed[i])\nend\n\n0.00 m/season: shoots   385 (observed 400), root:shoot 0.45 (observed 0.5)\n0.26 m/season: shoots   404 (observed 400), root:shoot 0.91 (observed 1.0)\n0.52 m/season: shoots   130 (observed 130), root:shoot 2.33 (observed 2.5)\n\nThis experiment constrains three traits:\n\nThe growth rate r: fitted to the 400 shoots of the 2 cm treatment.\nThe 4 cm treatment: the sand went onto the plants, and through the winter, when shoots do not elongate, it covered most of the canopy. The authors attribute the threefold loss of shoots to buried leaves. The model reproduces it through smothering, which fits the smothering time tau_sm.\nThe burial response below the optimum: fresh sand without burial cost only 4 ± 2 % of the shoots, so the direct burial response must be almost flat there.\n\nplot(; xlabel=\"days from 24 June 2016\", ylabel=\"shoots per mesocosm\", legend=:topleft)\nfor (i, s) in enumerate(e.solutions)\n    plot!(s.t, 6.25 .* s.N; lw=2, label=@sprintf(\"%.2f m/season\", e.burial[i]))\nend\nscatter!([365, 365, 365], e.shoots_observed; color=:black, label=\"observed\")\n\n(Image: )","title":"Burial in a mesocosm"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/13_marram_grass/#Burial-in-the-field","page":"Marram grass in one cell","text":"On a foredune planted in 2011, Nolet et al. (2018) related the change in NDVI and cover over the 2015 growing season to burial. They fitted Gaussian curves by quantile regression.\n\nThe model’s response is that of a stand buried at a constant rate for four years. Below the optimum it is lowered by the soil-biota pressure that builds up without burial. This fits the time scale tau_P and the burial d_f that relieves pressure. The two studies are fitted jointly: the mesocosm, on fresh sand, sees almost no pressure in one year, while the four-year-old planting sees half its vigour lost without burial.\n\ne = marram_experiment(:nolet2018)\nplot(e.burial, e.ndvi_observed; lw=1, color=:gray, alpha=0.7,\n     label=[\"NDVI quantiles 50, 90, 95, 99\" \"\" \"\" \"\"], xlabel=\"burial (m per season)\",\n     ylabel=\"response (relative to the peak)\")\nplot!(e.burial, e.response; lw=3, color=:black, label=\"model\")\n\n(Image: )","title":"Burial in the field"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/13_marram_grass/#Drought,-flooding-and-heat","page":"Marram grass in one cell","text":"Three more experiments test water and flooding:\n\nDrought: Dixon et al. (2004) withheld water for four weeks, and 80 % of marram plants recovered.\nFlooding: Aptekar and Rejmánek (2000) submerged rhizomes in seawater. Survival is the viability of their buds relative to the controls.\nHeat and drought: in a hot July without rain, the transplants of Lammers et al. (2024) were unaffected, although the top 10 cm of sand dried out. The model has no surface moisture. Its sparse planting transpires little, so the root zone stays moist.\n\ne = marram_experiment(:dixon2004)\n@printf(\"drought: survival %.2f (observed %.2f)\\n\", e.survival, e.survival_observed)\ne = marram_experiment(:aptekar2000)\nfor i in eachindex(e.days)\n    @printf(\"submerged %4.0f days: survival %.2f (observed %.2f)\\n\", e.days[i],\n            e.survival[i], e.survival_observed[i])\nend\ne = marram_experiment(:lammers2024)\n@printf(\"heat and drought: growth relative to ambient %.3f (observed 1, no difference)\\n\",\n        e.growth_ratio)\n\ndrought: survival 0.81 (observed 0.80)\nsubmerged    1 days: survival 1.00 (observed 0.85)\nsubmerged    3 days: survival 1.00 (observed 1.02)\nsubmerged    7 days: survival 0.80 (observed 0.80)\nsubmerged   13 days: survival 0.13 (observed 0.13)\nheat and drought: growth relative to ambient 0.999 (observed 1, no difference)\n\nA fourth, Homberger et al. (2024), grew marram in pots at seven levels of watering. It is a test the model only partly passes:\n\nDriest pots: plants died at a mean soil moisture of 3.3-4.8 % by volume. The model also kills them there, because that is below wilting.\nWetter pots: their plants grew more with every step of water, but the model’s stress function, from Homberger et al. (2026), is saturated above about 6 %.\n\nThe two papers’ moisture thresholds do not agree, and the model follows the 2026 values.\n\ne = marram_experiment(:homberger2024)\nfor i in eachindex(e.moisture)\n    @printf(\"moisture %4.1f %%: shoots x %.2f over 132 days\\n\", 100e.moisture[i], e.shoots[i])\nend\n\nmoisture  4.0 %: shoots x 0.03 over 132 days\nmoisture  6.2 %: shoots x 3.09 over 132 days\nmoisture  8.4 %: shoots x 3.09 over 132 days\nmoisture 10.6 %: shoots x 3.09 over 132 days\nmoisture 12.8 %: shoots x 3.09 over 132 days\nmoisture 15.0 %: shoots x 3.09 over 132 days\nmoisture 17.2 %: shoots x 3.09 over 132 days","title":"Drought, flooding and heat"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/13_marram_grass/#Ten-years-of-a-stand","page":"Marram grass in one cell","text":"A stand is followed through two kinds of change in its sand supply:\n\nYears 0-4: 0.3 m of sand per growing season, about the optimum.\nYears 4-7: no burial; the beach is cut off.\nYears 7-10: burial resumes.\n\nsimulate_marram records every multiplier, so the cause of each change can be read off.\n\nz(t) = (4 * 365 <= t < 7 * 365) ? 0.0 : 0.3 / A.season_length(tr)\nsol = simulate_marram(MarramCell(; N=50.0, h=0.4, Br=0.03, zr=0.3),\n                      (t, d) -> (; dzb=z(t), s=tr.s_fc), tr; days=3650,\n                      start=Date(2020, 1, 1))\nyr = sol.t ./ 365.25\np1 = plot(yr, sol.N; lw=2, legend=false, ylabel=\"shoots per m²\")\np2 = plot(yr, sol.P; lw=2, label=\"pressure P\", ylabel=\"state or multiplier\")\nplot!(p2, yr, sol.Gb; lw=2, label=\"burial G_b\")\np3 = plot(yr, sol.Br; lw=2, legend=false, ylabel=\"roots (kg/m²)\", xlabel=\"years\")\np4 = plot(yr, sol.cr; lw=2, legend=false, ylabel=\"reinforcement (kPa)\", xlabel=\"years\")\nplot(p1, p2, p3, p4; layout=(2, 2), size=(800, 600))\n\n(Image: )\n\nA few points about this run:\n\nWithout burial, pressure builds up and the stand thins, from about 420 to 310 shoots per m² in three years.\nRoots grow within weeks toward a target that rises with burial. Above the target they die back over years, so reinforcement falls more slowly than the target does.\nWhen sand returns, most of the pressure is relieved within months. The stand recovers most of its shoots in two years.\nReinforcement stays within the 0.5-5 kPa measured in dune sand.","title":"Ten years of a stand"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/13_marram_grass/#What-is-not-yet-known","page":"Marram grass in one cell","text":"These traits rest on little evidence and are reported with ranges in marram_info:\n\nBurial response: from one site, one growing season and one mesocosm; it was not confirmed on Ameland (Keijsers et al. 2015).\nPressure: its strength p_max and seed P_seed are priors. The fitted time scale depends on them.\nSmothering: the time scale is fitted to one treatment, assuming no elongation in winter.\nRoots: the tensile strength of marram roots has not been measured.\nFlooding: survival comes from rhizome fragments, not whole plants.\n\n<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Aptekar2000\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nAptekar, Rachel, and Marcel Rejmánek. 2000. “The Effect of Sea-Water Submergence on Rhizome Bud Viability of the Introduced Ammophila arenaria and the Native Leymus mollis in California.” Journal of Coastal Conservation 6: 107–11. https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02730474.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Dixon2004\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nDixon, Jennifer M., Michael J. Hilton, and Peter Bannister. 2004. “Desmoschoenus spiralis Displacement by Ammophila arenaria: The Role of Drought.” New Zealand Journal of Ecology 28 (2): 207–13. https://newzealandecology.org/nzje/2227.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Homberger2024\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nHomberger, Jan-Markus, Aaron Lynch, Michel Riksen, and Juul Limpens.\n\n“Growth Response of Dune-Building Grasses to Precipitation.”\n\nEcohydrology 18 (2): e2634. https://doi.org/10.1002/eco.2634.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Homberger2026\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nHomberger, Jan-Markus, Glenn Strypsteen, Abbey L. Marcotte, Sasja van Rosmalen, Michel Riksen, and Juul Limpens.\n\n“Plant-Water Interactions Shape Coastal Dune Evolution in a\n\nChanging Climate.” Journal of Hydrology 668: 134921. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhydrol.2026.134921.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Keijsers2015\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nKeijsers, Joep G. S., Alma V. De Groot, and Michel J. P. M. Riksen.\n\n“Vegetation and Sedimentation on Coastal Foredunes.”\n\nGeomorphology 228: 723–34. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.geomorph.2014.10.027.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Lammers2024\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nLammers, Carlijn, Paul M. J. Berghuis, Angeles G. Mayor, Valérie C. Reijers, Max Rietkerk, and Tjisse van der Heide. 2024. “Extreme Heat and Drought Did Not Affect Interspecific Interactions Between Dune Grasses.” Estuarine, Coastal and Shelf Science 311: 109020. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ecss.2024.109020.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Nolet2018\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nNolet, Corjan, Marinka van Puijenbroek, Juha Suomalainen, Juul Limpens, and Michel Riksen. 2018. “UAV-Imaging to Model Growth Response of Marram Grass to Sand Burial: Implications for Coastal Dune Development.” Aeolian Research 31: 50–61. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aeolia.2017.08.006.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Reijers2021\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nReijers, Valérie C., Selwyn Hoeks, Jim van Belzen, et al. 2021. “Sediment Availability Provokes a Shift from Brownian to L<span class=\"nocase\">évy</span>-Like Clonal Expansion in a Dune Building Grass.” Ecology Letters 24: 258–68. https://doi.org/10.1111/ele.13638.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-vanderPutten1993\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nvan der Putten, Wim H., Cor van Dijk, and B. A. M. Peters. 1993. “Plant-Specific Soil-Borne Diseases Contribute to Succession in Foredune Vegetation.” Nature 362: 53–56. https://doi.org/10.1038/362053a0.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-vanderStoel2002\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nvan der Stoel, Corine D., Wim H. van der Putten, and Henk Duyts. 2002. “Development of a Negative Plant-Soil Feedback in the Expansion Zone of the Clonal Grass Ammophila arenaria Following Root Formation and Nematode Colonization.” Journal of Ecology 90: 978–88. https://doi.org/10.1046/j.1365-2745.2002.00727.x.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"What is not yet known"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_configuration_files/#AeoLiS-configuration-files","page":"AeoLiS configuration files","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nReading a configuration file\nRunning it\nAgreement with aeolis-python\nWriting configuration files\nReferences","title":"AeoLiS configuration files"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_configuration_files/#Overview","page":"AeoLiS configuration files","text":"AeoLiS models are usually described by a configuration file, aeolis.txt, with one key = value line per parameter and input data in separate text files. Aeolis.jl reads these files, runs them as aeolis run does, and writes netCDF output in the AeoLiS layout. This vignette runs one of the AeoLiS regression cases, a transect with tides, waves, runup, vegetation and avalanching, and compares the result with aeolis-python (de Vries et al. 2023).\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing NCDatasets\nusing Plots","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_configuration_files/#Reading-a-configuration-file","page":"AeoLiS configuration files","text":"The case ships with the package tests. Its configuration file names the grid, bed, wind, tide and wave files, which read_config loads relative to the file’s directory. Keys that AeoLiS itself ignores (this file has six, such as process_nelayer) are skipped with a warning, which is hidden here.\n\ncase = joinpath(pkgdir(Aeolis), \"test\", \"data\", \"case1_small_waves\")\nreaddir(case)\n\n9-element Vector{String}:\n \"aeolis.txt\"\n \"reference.nc\"\n \"tide.txt\"\n \"veg.grd\"\n \"wave.txt\"\n \"wind.txt\"\n \"x.grd\"\n \"y.grd\"\n \"z.grd\"\n\np = read_config(joinpath(case, \"aeolis.txt\"))\n\nAeolisParameters\n  time: 0.0 to 172800.0 s, dt = 3600.0 s\n  scheme: euler_backward, solver: trunk\n  fractions: 1, layers: 3\n  enabled: process_wind, process_transport, process_bedupdate, process_threshold, th_grainsize, process_avalanche, process_tide, process_wave, process_runup, process_vegetation\n\nSpatial files hold one row per grid row, as in AeoLiS; they are stored with x as the first dimension. Time series keep their columns:\n\nsize(p.xgrid_file), size(p.wind_file), p.wind_file[1:3, :]\n\n((114,), (49, 3), [0.0 15.0 0.0; 3600.0 15.0 0.0; 7200.0 15.0 0.0])","title":"Reading a configuration file"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_configuration_files/#Running-it","page":"AeoLiS configuration files","text":"run_config solves the problem with the scheme and solver of the file and, because NCDatasets is loaded, writes the output file:\n\nout = joinpath(mktempdir(), \"aeolis.nc\")\nsol = run_config(joinpath(case, \"aeolis.txt\"); outputfile=out)\n\nAeolisSolution with 50 output times from 0.0 to 176400.0 s, retcode Success\n  saved: Ct, Hs, mass, qs, w, zb, zs\n\nThe netCDF file has the AeoLiS dimensions s, n, time, layers and fractions, with a transect written as a single row:\n\nNCDataset(out) do ds\n    [(k, size(ds[k])) for k in keys(ds) if k in (\"x\", \"zb\", \"mass\", \"Ct\", \"time\")]\nend\n\n5-element Vector{Tuple{String, Tuple{Int64, Vararg{Int64}}}}:\n (\"x\", (114, 1))\n (\"time\", (50,))\n (\"zb\", (114, 1, 50))\n (\"Ct\", (1, 114, 1, 50))\n (\"mass\", (1, 3, 114, 1, 50))\n\nzb = transect(sol, :zb)\nplot(sol.u.x[:, 2], zb[:, 1]; lw=2, label=\"initial\", xlabel=\"x (m)\", ylabel=\"bed level (m)\")\nplot!(sol.u.x[:, 2], zb[:, end]; lw=2, label=\"after $(sol.t[end] / 3600) h\")\n\n(Image: )","title":"Running it"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_configuration_files/#Agreement-with-aeolis-python","page":"AeoLiS configuration files","text":"The test data also hold reference.nc: the full model state of aeolis-python after every time step of the same case, in double precision. Stepping the Julia model alongside it compares every step:\n\nref = NCDataset(joinpath(case, \"reference.nc\"))\npyzb = Array(ref[\"zb\"])           # (x, y, step): netCDF's (step, y, x) reversed\ninteg = init(AeolisProblem(p); save_vars=Symbol[])\ndiffs = Float64[]\nfor k in 1:(size(pyzb, 3) - 1)\n    step!(integ)\n    push!(diffs, maximum(abs.(integ[:zb] .- pyzb[:, :, k + 1])))\nend\nclose(ref)\ndiffs\n\n6-element Vector{Float64}:\n 4.440892098500626e-16\n 4.440892098500626e-16\n 4.440892098500626e-16\n 4.440892098500626e-16\n 4.440892098500626e-16\n 4.440892098500626e-16\n\nThe largest difference in bed level over these steps is at the level of round-off:\n\nmaximum(diffs)\n\n4.440892098500626e-16","title":"Agreement with aeolis-python"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_configuration_files/#Writing-configuration-files","page":"AeoLiS configuration files","text":"write_config writes parameters back to a configuration file, with input arrays in text files next to it, so a model built in Julia can be run by aeolis-python:\n\ndir = mktempdir()\nwrite_config(joinpath(dir, \"aeolis.txt\"), p)\nfilter(f -> endswith(f, \".txt\"), readdir(dir))\n\n8-element Vector{String}:\n \"aeolis.txt\"\n \"bed.txt\"\n \"tide.txt\"\n \"veg.txt\"\n \"wave.txt\"\n \"wind.txt\"\n \"xgrid.txt\"\n \"ygrid.txt\"\n\nq = read_config(joinpath(dir, \"aeolis.txt\"))\nq.xgrid_file ≈ p.xgrid_file && q.wind_file ≈ p.wind_file && q.grain_size == p.grain_size\n\ntrue","title":"Writing configuration files"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/02_configuration_files/#References","page":"AeoLiS configuration files","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-deVries2023\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nde Vries, Sierd, Caroline Hallin, Christa van IJzendoorn, et al. 2023. AeoLiS. Computer software, version 3.0.1. https://github.com/openearth/aeolis-python.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api_processes/#Processes","page":"Processes","text":"These functions advance one process of a time step; AeolisProblem's integrator calls them in AeoLiS's order. They are not exported.","title":"Processes"},{"category":"section","location":"api_processes/#Numerical-helpers","page":"Processes","text":"","title":"Numerical helpers"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.setup_model","page":"Processes","text":"setup_model(p::AeolisParameters; T=Float64, rng=Random.Xoshiro(0))\n\nThe model and its initial state for the parameters p: the grid (a transect becomes a quasi-2-D grid of three identical rows, as in AeoLiS), grid spacing and orientation, bed composition, wind, vegetation and fences. Returns (model, state).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.setup_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.aeolis_step!","page":"Processes","text":"aeolis_step!(s, m, t, dt, scheme, solver, linsolve)\n\nAdvance the state s from time t by one AeoLiS update with requested step dt (-1 for the configured step). Returns the time step used, or nothing when no update was made.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.aeolis_step!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.set_timestep!","page":"Processes","text":"set_timestep!(m, s, dt, scheme)\n\nThe time step of the next update: dt when positive, otherwise the dt parameter, limited by the CFL condition for the explicit scheme and by max_bedlevel_change. Returns false for dt < -1, which skips |dt| seconds without computing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.set_timestep!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.initialize_grid!","page":"Processes","text":"initialize_grid!(s, m)\n\nCoordinates, cell sizes and areas, and the grid orientation m.alpha (AeoLiS gridparams.initialize). As in AeoLiS, dn is the distance between neighbouring points along x and ds along y; the two are equal on square grids and on transects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.initialize_grid!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.grid_rotate!","page":"Processes","text":"grid_rotate!(s, m, angle)\n\nRotate the coordinates and every vector field (shear stress, shear velocity, wind, fluxes, grain velocities) by angle degrees, and add angle to the wind direction (AeoLiS grid_rotate).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.grid_rotate!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.initialize_wind!","page":"Processes","text":"initialize_wind!(s, m)\n\nPrepare the shear computation for two-dimensional grids (transects are quasi-2-D, so they count): the spectral WindShear for method_shear = :fft, a rotated grid for :quasi2d and :duna2d.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.initialize_wind!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.interpolate_wind!","page":"Processes","text":"interpolate_wind!(s, m, t)\n\nWind speed and direction at time t (the value at the last time not after t, repeating the series beyond its end; directions through their sine and cosine), its components on the grid, and the shear velocity and stress of the undisturbed wind over a bed of roughness z0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.interpolate_wind!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.roughness_length","page":"Processes","text":"roughness_length(s, m)\n\nThe roughness length z0 for method_roughness: :constant (z0 = k, as AeoLiS for backward compatibility), :constant_nikuradse (k/30), :mean_grainsize_initial, :mean_grainsize_adaptive, :median_grainsize_adaptive (Sherman and Greenwood 1982) or :vanrijn_strypsteen (van Rijn and Strypsteen 2019). A scalar or a grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.roughness_length"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.velocity_stress!","page":"Processes","text":"velocity_stress!(s, m)\n\nShear stress from shear velocity: tau = rhoa * ustar^2, with components along the shear velocity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.velocity_stress!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.stress_velocity!","page":"Processes","text":"stress_velocity!(s, m)\n\nShear velocity from shear stress: ustar = sqrt(tau / rhoa), with components along the stress.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.stress_velocity!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.compute_shear!","page":"Processes","text":"compute_shear!(s, m)\n\nPerturb the shear stress by the topography (AeoLiS wind.shear): the spectral method of Weng et al. (1991) with a separation bubble for method_shear = :fft, or the rotated-grid methods :quasi2d (Kroy et al. 2002) and :duna2d along wind-aligned transects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.compute_shear!"},{"category":"type","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.WindShear","page":"Processes","text":"WindShear(dx, dy, L, l, z0, buffer_width)\n\nThe settings of the spectral shear computation: computational cell sizes, the dune length scale L, the inner layer height l, the roughness length z0 (fixed at its initial value, as in AeoLiS) and the width of the buffer around the rotated grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.WindShear"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.wind_shear!","page":"Processes","text":"wind_shear!(sh, x, y, z, taux, tauy, u0, udir, separation, c, mu_b, taus0, taun0,\n            sep_filter_iterations, zsep_y_filter)\n\nThe shear stress components over the bed z and the height of the separation bubble, on the input grid (AeoLiS WindShear.__call__). Returns (taux, tauy, hsep).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.wind_shear!"},{"category":"type","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.RotationGrid","page":"Processes","text":"RotationGrid(dx, dy, buffer_width)\n\nA wind-aligned computational grid with cells dx by dy and a buffer around the input grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.RotationGrid"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.rotated_shear!","page":"Processes","text":"rotated_shear!(rg, s, m)\n\nShear stress perturbed along wind-aligned transects by method_shear (:duna2d, the Duna integral; :quasi2d, Kroy et al. 2002), with the quasi-2-D separation bubble. Returns (tau, zsep, hsep) on the model grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.rotated_shear!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.okin_shear!","page":"Processes","text":"okin_shear!(rg, s, m, c1, intercept)\n\nThe shear velocity reduced in the lee of vegetation (Okin 2008) along wind-aligned rows, interpolated back to the model grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.okin_shear!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.compute_threshold!","page":"Processes","text":"compute_threshold!(s, m)\n\nThe shear velocity threshold of every cell and fraction from the enabled th_* processes, the threshold mask, and the non-erodible layer. With threshold_file the threshold is fixed (only the non-erodible layer applies).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.compute_threshold!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.threshold_grainsize!","page":"Processes","text":"threshold_grainsize!(s, m)\n\nBagnold's threshold Aa * sqrt((rhog - rhoa) / rhoa * g * d) for every fraction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.threshold_grainsize!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.threshold_moisture!","page":"Processes","text":"threshold_moisture!(s, m)\n\nRaise the threshold with the gravimetric moisture content by method_moist_threshold, and make it infinite above max_moist percent volumetric moisture.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.threshold_moisture!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.threshold_salt!","page":"Processes","text":"threshold_salt!(s, m)\n\nRaise the threshold with the salt content of the top layer (Nickling and Ecclestone 1981).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.threshold_salt!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.threshold_sheltering!","page":"Processes","text":"threshold_sheltering!(s, m)\n\nRaise the threshold by sheltering from non-erodible roughness elements (Raupach et al. 1993), from the share of top-layer mass in fractions that the wind cannot move.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.threshold_sheltering!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.threshold_nelayer!","page":"Processes","text":"threshold_nelayer!(s, m)\n\nMake the threshold infinite where the bed is within 0.01 m of the non-erodible layer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.threshold_nelayer!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.grainspeed_duran","page":"Processes","text":"grainspeed_duran(s, m)\n\nGrain velocities after Duran et al. (2011), with the effect of bed slopes; also the velocity over a flat bed and the SedTRAILS velocities (zero where the wind is below the threshold). Returns (u0, us, un, u, usST, unST), each (nx+1, ny+1, nfractions).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.grainspeed_duran"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.grainspeed_constant!","page":"Processes","text":"grainspeed_constant!(s, m)\n\nGrain velocity of 1 m/s along the shear velocity (or along the wind where the shear velocity is zero).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.grainspeed_constant!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.equilibrium!","page":"Processes","text":"equilibrium!(s, m)\n\nGrain velocities and the saturated sediment concentration Cu (and Cuf with the fluid threshold, Cu0 over a flat bed) by method_transport: :bagnold, :bagnold_gs, :kawamura, :lettau, :dk, :sauermann or :vanrijn_strypsteen, multiplied by accfac.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.equilibrium!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.compute_weights","page":"Processes","text":"compute_weights(s, m)\n\nWeights of the fractions: from the concentrations in the air relative to saturation (w_air), from the top layer of the bed (w_bed), and their combination by the bed interaction factor bi, normalised. Returns (w, w_air, w_bed).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.compute_weights"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.renormalize_weights!","page":"Processes","text":"renormalize_weights!(w, ix)\n\nRescale the weights of fractions ix and above so that all weights sum to one; cells whose weights are all zero get equal weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.renormalize_weights!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.solve_trunk","page":"Processes","text":"solve_trunk(s, m, dt, alpha, beta, linsolve)\n\nThe default AeoLiS solver: an implicit (alpha = 1), Crank-Nicolson (alpha = 0.5) or explicit upwind discretisation solved as a sparse linear system per fraction, iterating the fraction weights until the pickup is supported by the bed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.solve_trunk"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.solve_euler_forward","page":"Processes","text":"solve_euler_forward(s, m, dt)\n\nThe explicit AeoLiS solver (solve_EF): first-order upwind advection on a grid with ghost cells (the first cross-shore column and the ghost column are empty, the lateral edges wrap) plus the pickup limited by the available sediment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.solve_euler_forward"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.solve_pieter","page":"Processes","text":"solve_pieter(s, m, dt, alpha, beta, linsolve)\n\nThe finite-volume AeoLiS solver (solve_pieter): upwind fluxes on cell faces, the change of concentration solved for iteratively with erosion limited by the bed, and flux, constant, gradient or circular boundaries. At the first step (t == 0) only the weights are set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.solve_pieter"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.solve_steady_state","page":"Processes","text":"solve_steady_state(s, m, dt)\n\nThe steady-state AeoLiS solver (solve_SS with sweep): the concentration in equilibrium with the current wind, found by sweeping the grid in the four upwind directions, with the pickup limited by the top layer; circular boundaries are iterated to convergence.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.solve_steady_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.sweep!","page":"Processes","text":"sweep!(Ct, Cu, mass, dt, Ts, ds, dn, us, un, w)\n\nAeoLiS's steady-state sweep. Boundary types are passed in Ct's first fraction as in AeoLiS (-1 on the edges for circular, -2 for re-circulating). Indices below follow the Python code (0-based n along y, s along x) and are converted at each access.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.sweep!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.initialize_bed!","page":"Processes","text":"initialize_bed!(s, m)\n\nBed level, non-erodible layer, layer thickness and the sediment mass per layer and fraction from grain_dist (one distribution, or one row per layer), bedcomp_file or supply_file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.initialize_bed!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.mixtoplayer!","page":"Processes","text":"mixtoplayer!(s, m)\n\nMix the layers within the depth of disturbance (facDOD times the wave height for mixing) when process_mixtoplayer is on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.mixtoplayer!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.wet_bed_reset!","page":"Processes","text":"wet_bed_reset!(s, m)\n\nRelax the bed below the total water level towards its initial level with time scale Tbedreset when process_wet_bed_reset is on.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.wet_bed_reset!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.update_bed!","page":"Processes","text":"update_bed!(s, m)\n\nRemove the pickup from the top layer, shift mass between layers so that layer thicknesses are kept, take new sediment from below the bottom layer with the initial composition, and change the bed level by the net deposition (AeoLiS bed.update). Like AeoLiS this may modify s.pickup where it would remove more sediment than available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.update_bed!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.average_change!","page":"Processes","text":"average_change!(s, m)\n\nBed level change over the step, per year, and averaged over avg_time (AeoLiS average_change); dzbveg is zero until avg_time has passed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.average_change!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.interpolate_hydro!","page":"Processes","text":"interpolate_hydro!(s, m, t)\n\nStill and total water levels, wave height and period, the wave height for mixing, and the meteorological conditions at time t; the water level zs is at least the bed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.interpolate_hydro!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.update_hydro!","page":"Processes","text":"update_hydro!(s, m, dt, t)\n\nGroundwater (fourth-order Runge-Kutta on the Boussinesq equation in sub-steps of dt / tfac_gw, with runup overheight), moisture (:infiltration or :surf_moisture with soil water retention, optionally with scanning curves), and salt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.update_hydro!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.stockdon_runup","page":"Processes","text":"stockdon_runup(Ho, Tp, beta)\n\nSetup, swash and runup (eta, sigma_s, R) of waves of height Ho and period Tp on a beach of slope beta (Stockdon et al. 2006, as in AeoLiS).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.stockdon_runup"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.angle_of_repose!","page":"Processes","text":"angle_of_repose!(s, m)\n\nSet the dynamic angle of repose of every cell to theta_dyn.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.angle_of_repose!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.avalanche!","page":"Processes","text":"avalanche!(s, m)\n\nRedistribute sand down slopes steeper than the angle of repose (an explicit iteration with a relaxation factor 0.1, at most max_iter_ava sweeps; the downslope gradient of a cell is the steeper one-sided difference, zero on the grid edges and at local minima; fluxes wrap around the grid). The water level is reset to at least the bed; like AeoLiS, the still water level then takes the same values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.avalanche!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.initialize_vegetation!","page":"Processes","text":"initialize_vegetation!(s, m)\n\nVegetation cover from veg_file (negative values set to zero), height hveg_max * sqrt(cover), and the vegetated cells. With veg_model = marram, marram grass is set up from the cover (initialize_marram!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.initialize_vegetation!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.vegetation_shear!","page":"Processes","text":"vegetation_shear!(s, m)\n\nReduce the shear velocity by vegetation: Raupach's factor 1 / sqrt(1 + gamma_vegshear * cover), optionally smoothed by a Gaussian filter of width veg_sigma, or Okin's reduction in the lee of each plant on a wind-aligned grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.vegetation_shear!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.germinate!","page":"Processes","text":"germinate!(s, m)\n\nGermination where the bed is not eroding, and lateral expansion from vegetated neighbours, with yearly probabilities germinate and lateral scaled to the time step. Uses the model's random number generator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.germinate!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.grow_vegetation!","page":"Processes","text":"grow_vegetation!(s, m)\n\nVertical growth towards hveg_max at rate V_ver, reduced by burial or erosion away from dzb_opt; plants are removed where flooded or below veg_min_elevation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.grow_vegetation!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.initialize_fences!","page":"Processes","text":"initialize_fences!(s, m)\n\nFence heights from fence_file and the level of their tops above the initial bed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.initialize_fences!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.update_fences!","page":"Processes","text":"update_fences!(s, m)\n\nThe exposed fence heights (fences lower than 0.1 m or higher than 1.5 m are ignored), and the shear velocity reduced in their lee.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.update_fences!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.dune_erosion!","page":"Processes","text":"dune_erosion!(s, m, t)\n\nErode the dune face along each transect where the total water level (runup times 1.26 plus the tide) exceeds dune_toe_elevation: the eroded volume 4 Cs (TWL - z_toe)^2 Nc, with Nc the number of collisions in the step, is removed by cutting the profile back to a slope of 0.54 beach_slope from the toe.\n\nWith marram grass (veg_model = marram), roots resist the cut: removing sand from a cell's rooted layer (veg_zr) costs 1 + veg_cr / tau0 times as much of the wave demand as bare sand, where tau0 is the strength of moist bare sand (Erikson et al. (2007)). Sand below the rooted layer erodes as bare sand. For a uniform reinforcement deeper than the cut, this is the erosion coefficient Cs tau0 / (tau0 + c_r). Vegetated dunes that erode faster through run-up and infiltration, as in the flume of Feagin et al. (2023), are not represented.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.dune_erosion!"},{"category":"type","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.InputArray","page":"Processes","text":"InputArray\n\nType of the parameters that hold input data (grids, time series, masks): nothing or an array. In a configuration file they name a file, which read_config loads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.InputArray"},{"category":"type","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.AeolisModel","page":"Processes","text":"AeolisModel{T}\n\nEverything a time step needs besides the state: the parameters, the grid dimensions, the forcing series, masks, caches for shear computations, the random number generator and counters. Built by AeolisProblem's integrator; users normally reach it as integ.model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.AeolisModel"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.state_dims","page":"Processes","text":"state_dims(name, nx, ny, nlayers, nfractions)\n\nThe size of the state variable name on a grid of nx by ny cells (nx+1 by ny+1 points).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.state_dims"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.copyto_state!","page":"Processes","text":"copyto_state!(dst::AeolisState, src::AeolisState)\n\nCopy every variable of src into dst.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.copyto_state!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.parse_value","page":"Processes","text":"parse_value(val; dir=\".\", key=nothing)\n\nThe value of a configuration entry: everything after % is a comment; a value with spaces is a list; T/F are booleans; None and an empty value are nothing; the name of an existing file (relative to dir) is loaded with load_array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.parse_value"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.rotate","page":"Processes","text":"rotate(x, y, alpha; origin=(0, 0))\n\nRotate the points (x, y) by alpha degrees about origin, in the sense AeoLiS uses: the row vector [x y] is multiplied by [cos a -sin a; sin a cos a], so a positive angle turns the points clockwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.rotate"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.rotate!","page":"Processes","text":"rotate!(x, y, alpha; origin=(0, 0))\n\nrotate in place: x and y are overwritten by the rotated points, with the same arithmetic and so the same values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.rotate!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.normalize_along","page":"Processes","text":"normalize_along(x, dim; ref=nothing, fill=0.0)\n\nx divided by its sum along dim (or by ref), with fill where the divisor is zero (numpy-style normalize of AeoLiS).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.normalize_along"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.prevent_tiny_negatives!","page":"Processes","text":"prevent_tiny_negatives!(x, max_error=1e-10, replacement=0.0)\n\nReplace the values of x in (-max_error, 0) by replacement.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.prevent_tiny_negatives!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.apply_mask!","page":"Processes","text":"apply_mask!(arr, mask)\n\nMultiply arr by the real part of the complex mask and add its imaginary part. A mask covering the first dimensions of arr is repeated over the rest; a scalar mask applies to every cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.apply_mask!"},{"category":"type","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.CircularSeries","page":"Processes","text":"CircularSeries(t, values)\n\nA linear interpolant of a time series that repeats beyond its range the way AeoLiS's interp_circular does: a time x maps to tmin + mod(x - tmax - 1, tmax - tmin + 1) and is then interpolated linearly, clamped at the ends (numpy.interp).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.CircularSeries"},{"category":"type","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.CircularStepSeries","page":"Processes","text":"CircularStepSeries(t, values)\n\nThe value of a time series at the last time not after x, repeating with period tmax - tmin (AeoLiS's interp_circular_nearest, used for wind).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.CircularStepSeries"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.clamped_interp","page":"Processes","text":"clamped_interp(x, xp, fp)\n\nLinear interpolation clamped at the ends (numpy.interp).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.clamped_interp"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.diags","page":"Processes","text":"diags(diagonals, offsets, n)\n\nAn n by n sparse matrix with diagonals[k] on diagonal offsets[k], placed as scipy.sparse.diags places them: for an offset d >= 0 entry i goes to (i, i+d), for d < 0 to (i-d, i). The AeoLiS solvers rely on this placement, including where a diagonal is taken from a shifted copy of a coefficient array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.diags"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.np_arange","page":"Processes","text":"np_arange(start, stop, step)\n\nThe points of numpy.arange(start, stop, step) for floats: ceil((stop - start) / step) points start + i * step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.np_arange"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.exact_grid","page":"Processes","text":"exact_grid(xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax, dx, dy)\n\nThe regular grid of AeoLiS's get_exact_grid, as (x, y) arrays of size (nx, ny).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.exact_grid"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.fftfreq","page":"Processes","text":"fftfreq(n, d)\n\nThe sample frequencies of numpy.fft.fftfreq(n, d).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.fftfreq"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.pad_odd_reflect","page":"Processes","text":"pad_odd_reflect(v, w)\n\nv padded by w points at each end with numpy.pad(..., 'reflect', reflect_type='odd'), which reflects repeatedly when w exceeds length(v) - 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.pad_odd_reflect"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.pad_edge","page":"Processes","text":"pad_edge(a, w)\n\nThe matrix a padded by w cells on every side with its edge values (numpy.pad with 'edge').\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.pad_edge"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.regular_grid_interp","page":"Processes","text":"regular_grid_interp(xg, yg, z, xq, yq, fill)\n\nBilinear interpolation of z[i, j] given at (xg[i], yg[j]) to the points (xq, yq), with fill outside the grid, as scipy.interpolate.RegularGridInterpolator with bounds_error=False does: the cell is found with a left-sided search, clamped to the last interval.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.regular_grid_interp"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.linear_fit","page":"Processes","text":"linear_fit(x, y)\n\nSlope and intercept of the least-squares line through the points (numpy.polyfit(x, y, 1)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.linear_fit"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.max_diff","page":"Processes","text":"max_diff(arr)\n\nThe largest abs(arr[i] - arr[j]) + abs(i - j) over pairs (AeoLiS's maxDiff).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.max_diff"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.gaussian_filter1d","page":"Processes","text":"gaussian_filter1d(a, sigma, dim)\n\nGaussian filter of a along dimension dim with scipy.ndimage.gaussian_filter1d semantics (mode='reflect', truncate=4). A zero sigma returns a copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.gaussian_filter1d"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.gaussian_filter","page":"Processes","text":"gaussian_filter(a, sigma)\n\nGaussian filter of a along every dimension (scipy.ndimage.gaussian_filter).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.gaussian_filter"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.grain_size_percentile","page":"Processes","text":"grain_size_percentile(p, mass, percent)\n\nGrain size at percent of the cumulative mass of the top layer in every cell (AeoLiS's calc_grain_size), interpolated linearly and extrapolated beyond the coarsest fraction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.grain_size_percentile"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.mean_grain_size","page":"Processes","text":"mean_grain_size(grain_size, mass)\n\nMass-weighted mean grain size of the top layer in every cell.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.mean_grain_size"},{"category":"function","location":"api_processes/#Aeolis.pyrange","page":"Processes","text":"pyrange(n, start, stop)\n\nThe 1-based indices of the Python slice start:stop of a sequence of length n, with negative bounds counting from the end, as numpy slices them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.pyrange"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_transport_formulations/#Transport-formulations","page":"Transport formulations","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nShear velocity and its threshold\nSaturated flux\nGrain velocity\nReferences","title":"Transport formulations"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_transport_formulations/#Overview","page":"Transport formulations","text":"The saturated sediment flux is a function of the shear velocity u* and its threshold u*t. AeoLiS offers several classic formulations of it, of which Bagnold’s is the default (Bagnold 1937; van Rijn and Strypsteen 2020), and several ways to compute the grain velocity, including the grain-scale model of Durán et al. (2011). This vignette compares them by running the model on a long flat transect, where the flux downwind is saturated.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing Plots","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_transport_formulations/#Shear-velocity-and-its-threshold","page":"Transport formulations","text":"The wind at height z gives the shear velocity through the law of the wall with roughness length z0 (by default z0 = k, as in AeoLiS for backward compatibility). The threshold from grain size is Bagnold’s Aa sqrt((rhog - rhoa) / rhoa g d):\n\np = AeolisParameters()\nustar(u) = u * p.kappa / log(p.z / p.k)\nuth(d) = p.Aa * sqrt((p.rhog - p.rhoa) / p.rhoa * p.g * d)\nd = range(0.1e-3, 1.0e-3; length=50)\nplot(1000 .* d, uth.(d); lw=2, xlabel=\"grain size (mm)\", ylabel=\"threshold u*t (m/s)\",\n     label=\"\")\n\n(Image: )\n\nA grain of 0.3 mm starts to move at a wind speed (at 10 m) of\n\nuth(0.3e-3) / p.kappa * log(p.z / p.k)\n\n4.816819474863292","title":"Shear velocity and its threshold"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_transport_formulations/#Saturated-flux","page":"Transport formulations","text":"A helper runs a 100 m flat transect for half an hour and returns the flux at its downwind end:\n\nfunction saturated_flux(u; kwargs...)\n    x = collect(0.0:1.0:100.0)\n    q = AeolisParameters(; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=fill(2.0, length(x)),\n                         wind_file=[0.0 u 270.0; 86400.0 u 270.0], grain_size=0.0003,\n                         dt=300.0, tstop=1800.0, output_times=1800.0, output_vars=[:qs],\n                         kwargs...)\n    sol = solve(AeolisProblem(q))\n    return transect(sol, :qs)[end - 1, 1, end]\nend\nsaturated_flux(10.0)\n\n0.002300579301885339\n\nspeeds = 4.0:1.0:16.0\nplt = plot(; xlabel=\"wind speed at 10 m (m/s)\", ylabel=\"saturated flux (kg/m/s)\",\n           legend=:topleft)\nfor m in (:bagnold, :kawamura, :lettau, :dk, :sauermann, :vanrijn_strypsteen)\n    plot!(plt, speeds, [saturated_flux(u; method_transport=m) for u in speeds];\n          lw=2, label=String(m))\nend\nplt\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe formulations agree on a threshold and a steep rise above it; they differ by factors of two or more at high wind speed, which is why the transport constant (Cb, Ck, Cl, Cdk) is often calibrated.","title":"Saturated flux"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_transport_formulations/#Grain-velocity","page":"Transport formulations","text":"The flux is the concentration times the grain velocity. With method_grainspeed = :windspeed grains move at the wind speed; :duran uses the grain-scale model of Duran et al., including the effect of bed slopes; :constant sets 1 m/s. Since the saturated concentration is the saturated flux divided by the grain velocity, the flux of the Bagnold formulation is the same for every grain velocity, but the concentration and the adaptation length are not:\n\nfunction saturated_state(u, method)\n    x = collect(0.0:1.0:100.0)\n    q = AeolisParameters(; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=fill(2.0, length(x)),\n                         wind_file=[0.0 u 270.0; 86400.0 u 270.0], grain_size=0.0003,\n                         dt=300.0, tstop=1800.0, output_times=1800.0,\n                         output_vars=[:qs, :Ct, :u], method_grainspeed=method)\n    sol = solve(AeolisProblem(q))\n    return (; method, flux=transect(sol, :qs)[end - 1, 1, end],\n            concentration=transect(sol, :Ct)[end - 1, 1, end],\n            grain_velocity=transect(sol, :u)[end - 1, 1, end])\nend\n[saturated_state(12.0, m) for m in (:windspeed, :duran, :constant)]\n\n3-element Vector{@NamedTuple{method::Symbol, flux::Float64, concentration::Float64, grain_velocity::Float64}}:\n (method = :windspeed, flux = 0.0061217561693647045, concentration = 0.0005101463474470587, grain_velocity = 12.0)\n (method = :duran, flux = 0.006123972245587834, concentration = 0.004424653136204793, grain_velocity = 1.3840570225671107)\n (method = :constant, flux = 0.006123972245587831, concentration = 0.006123972245587831, grain_velocity = 1.0)","title":"Grain velocity"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/03_transport_formulations/#References","page":"Transport formulations","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Bagnold1937\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nBagnold, R. A. 1937. “The Transport of Sand by Wind.” The Geographical Journal 89: 409–38. https://doi.org/10.2307/1786411.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Duran2011\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nDurán, O., P. Claudin, and B. Andreotti. 2011. “On Aeolian Transport: Grain-Scale Interactions, Dynamical Mechanisms and Scaling Laws.” Aeolian Research 3: 243–70. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aeolia.2011.07.006.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-vanRijn2020\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nvan Rijn, L. C., and G. Strypsteen. 2020. “A Fully Predictive Model for Aeolian Sand Transport.” Coastal Engineering 156: 103600. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coastaleng.2019.103600.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Problems-and-solutions","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"","title":"Problems and solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Exceptions","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"","title":"Exceptions"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#Aeolis","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"Aeolis\n\nA Julia port of AeoLiS, the process-based model of supply-limited aeolian sediment transport (de Vries et al. 2023): wind and its perturbation by topography, the shear velocity threshold with moisture, salt, sheltering and non-erodible layers, equilibrium transport, multiple sediment fractions in bed layers, tides, waves, runup, groundwater and moisture, avalanching, vegetation, sand fences and dune erosion.\n\nA model is an AeolisProblem built from AeolisParameters (or an AeoLiS configuration file, read_config) and solved with the SciML interface (init, step!, solve) by a scheme (EulerBackward, CrankNicolson, EulerForward) and a transport solver (Trunk, Pieter, SteadyState). Loading NCDatasets enables write_netcdf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.AeolisProblem","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"AeolisProblem(p::AeolisParameters; tspan=(p.tstart, p.tstop), u0=nothing)\nAeolisProblem(configfile::AbstractString; kwargs...)\n\nAn AeoLiS simulation from tspan[1] to tspan[2]. Like AeoLiS, steps are taken while the time is at most tspan[2]. u0 replaces the initial state built from the parameters. A configuration file is read with read_config.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.AeolisProblem"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CommonSolve.init-Tuple{AeolisProblem, AbstractAeolisAlgorithm}","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"init(prob::AeolisProblem, alg=default_algorithm(prob.p); saveat=prob.p.output_times,\n     save_vars=prob.p.output_vars, save_stats=prob.p.output_types, callback=nothing,\n     tstops=Float64[], dt=-1, fixed=Dict(), restart=nothing, restart_every=prob.p.restart,\n     restart_file=nothing, rng=Random.Xoshiro(0), T=Float64)\n\nAn AeolisIntegrator for prob. Output follows AeoLiS: a save whenever at least saveat seconds have passed since the last one (and at t == 0); a vector of times saves at those times, which are added to tstops. save_vars are state variables, optionally with a statistic suffix (:zb_avg); save_stats adds statistics to every saved variable. dt is the requested step (-1 for the dt parameter). Variables in fixed keep the given values (AeoLiS hotstart files). restart is a file written by save_restart.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.init"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CommonSolve.solve-Tuple{AeolisProblem, AbstractAeolisAlgorithm}","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"solve(prob::AeolisProblem, alg=default_algorithm(prob.p); kwargs...)\n\nRun the simulation and return an AeolisSolution; keywords as for init.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.solve"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CommonSolve.step!-Tuple{AeolisIntegrator, Integer}","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"step!(integ::AeolisIntegrator, n=1)\n\nTake n AeoLiS updates, saving output and applying callbacks after each.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.step!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CommonSolve.solve!-Tuple{AeolisIntegrator}","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"solve!(integ::AeolisIntegrator)\n\nStep while the time is at most the end of the time span, then return the AeolisSolution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CommonSolve.solve!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SciMLBase.remake-Tuple{AeolisProblem}","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"remake(prob::AeolisProblem; p=prob.p, tspan=prob.tspan, u0=prob.u0, kwargs...)\n\nA copy of prob with parts replaced; other keywords replace parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SciMLBase.remake"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#SciMLBase.reinit!-Tuple{AeolisIntegrator}","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"reinit!(integ::AeolisIntegrator)\n\nRestart the integrator from the initial state of its problem, clearing saved output.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"SciMLBase.reinit!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.AeolisIntegrator","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"AeolisIntegrator\n\nThe state of a running simulation, from init(prob, alg): the state u (an AeolisState), the time t, the last step dt, the model with parameters model.p, and the saved output. integ[:zb] reads a state variable; step! advances it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.AeolisIntegrator"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.AeolisSolution","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"AeolisSolution\n\nThe result of solving an AeolisProblem: output times t, and for every saved variable an array with time as the last dimension. sol[:zb] gives the bed level at every output time, sol[:zb, k] at the k-th; statistics are named :zb_avg, :zb_max and so on. sol.u is the final state, sol.model the model, sol.retcode a SciMLBase ReturnCode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.AeolisSolution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Aeolis.transect","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"transect(sol, name)\n\nFor a transect (a quasi-2-D grid of three rows), the saved variable name along the middle row, as AeoLiS writes it: an array of size (nx+1, ..., ntimes).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.transect"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Aeolis.statistic","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"statistic(integ, name, stat)\n\nThe running statistic stat (:avg, :sum, :var, :min or :max) of state variable name since the last output, as AeoLiS's get_statistic (:sum is multiplied by the last time step).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.statistic"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Aeolis.save_restart","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"save_restart(integ, file)\n\nWrite the time, state and counters of integ to file (Julia serialization), from which init(prob; restart=file) continues.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.save_restart"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.AbstractAeolisAlgorithm","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"AbstractAeolisAlgorithm\n\nSupertype of the numerical schemes: EulerBackward, CrankNicolson and EulerForward, each with a transport solver and an optional LinearSolve.jl algorithm for the sparse systems.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.AbstractAeolisAlgorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.EulerBackward","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"EulerBackward(solver=Trunk(); linsolve=nothing)\n\nThe implicit Euler (AeoLiS default) scheme with transport solver solver. linsolve is a LinearSolve.jl algorithm for the sparse systems (nothing lets LinearSolve choose).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.EulerBackward"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.CrankNicolson","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"CrankNicolson(solver=Trunk(); linsolve=nothing)\n\nThe Crank-Nicolson scheme with transport solver solver. linsolve is a LinearSolve.jl algorithm for the sparse systems (nothing lets LinearSolve choose).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.CrankNicolson"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.EulerForward","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"EulerForward(solver=Trunk(); linsolve=nothing)\n\nThe explicit Euler, with the time step limited by the CFL number scheme with transport solver solver. linsolve is a LinearSolve.jl algorithm for the sparse systems (nothing lets LinearSolve choose).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.EulerForward"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Aeolis.default_algorithm","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"default_algorithm(p::AeolisParameters)\n\nThe algorithm named by the scheme and solver parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.default_algorithm"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.AbstractTransportSolver","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"AbstractTransportSolver\n\nSupertype of the transport solvers used inside a scheme: Trunk, Pieter and SteadyState.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.AbstractTransportSolver"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.Trunk","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"Trunk()\n\nThe default AeoLiS transport solver: an upwind discretisation solved as a sparse linear system per fraction (solver = trunk).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.Trunk"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.Pieter","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"Pieter()\n\nThe finite-volume AeoLiS transport solver with fluxes on cell faces (solver = pieter).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.Pieter"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.SteadyState","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"SteadyState()\n\nThe steady-state AeoLiS transport solver, sweeping the grid upwind (solver = steadystate).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.SteadyState"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Aeolis.read_config","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"read_config(file; kwargs...)\n\nThe AeolisParameters of an AeoLiS configuration file, with files loaded relative to the file's directory. Keywords override entries of the file. Keys that AeoLiS ignores (and nfractions, which follows from grain_size) are skipped with a warning listing them. output_file defaults to the configuration file name with extension .nc, and h to z.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.read_config"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Aeolis.write_config","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"write_config(file, p::AeolisParameters)\n\nWrite p as an AeoLiS configuration file. Input arrays are written to text files next to it (bed_file to bed.txt, and so on) in the layout AeoLiS reads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.write_config"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Aeolis.run_config","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"run_config(file; outputfile=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nRun the AeoLiS model of a configuration file, like aeolis run file: solve with the scheme and solver of the file and, if NCDatasets is loaded, write the output to outputfile (the file's output_file). Returns the AeolisSolution. Keywords go to solve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.run_config"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Aeolis.write_netcdf","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"write_netcdf(file, sol::AeolisSolution)\n\nWrite the saved output of sol to a netCDF file with the variables, dimensions and attributes of AeoLiS output (transects as one row). Needs NCDatasets.jl to be loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.write_netcdf"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Aeolis.load_array","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"load_array(file; spatial=false)\n\nThe numbers in a text file as AeoLiS's numpy.loadtxt reads them: a vector for a single row or column, otherwise a matrix (transposed for spatial files, so that x is the first dimension). Complex numbers are allowed (masks).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.load_array"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.AeolisError","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"AeolisError\n\nAbstract supertype of every exception that Aeolis defines. Invalid arguments and keywords raise Base's ArgumentError instead, which is outside this root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.AeolisError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.ConfigurationError","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"ConfigurationError(message)\n\nRaised when the parameters do not describe a runnable model, for example when the grid or bed is missing or an input series has too few columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.ConfigurationError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.UnknownOptionError","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"UnknownOptionError(parameter, value, allowed)\n\nRaised when a method or boundary parameter names an option that does not exist; allowed lists the options that do.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.UnknownOptionError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#Aeolis.UnsupportedOptionError","page":"Problems and solutions","text":"UnsupportedOptionError(parameter, value, reason)\n\nRaised when an option exists in AeoLiS but has no implementation there either (for example a cross-shore circular boundary or the dry-layer threshold), or is not available in this port.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.UnsupportedOptionError"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/14_marram_foredune/#A-foredune-built-by-marram-grass","page":"A foredune built by marram grass","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nThe beach and its weather\nThe dune\nThe plants\nHow densely to plant\nA storm\nWhat remains uncertain","title":"A foredune built by marram grass"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/14_marram_foredune/#Overview","page":"A foredune built by marram grass","text":"With veg_model = marram, every grid cell carries the marram grass model of the previous vignette, and the plants and the bed are coupled both ways:\n\nPlants shape the wind. Each time step, shoot density and canopy height give a frontal area. That frontal area becomes the effective cover in AeoLiS’s shear reduction, 1 / sqrt(1 + gamma_vegshear rhoveg). The shoots bend in strong wind, so the cover depends on the current wind speed (Laporte-Fauret et al. 2024; Walter et al. 2012).\nThe bed shapes the plants. Every veg_interval (a day), each cell is advanced using:\nthe rate of bed-level change since the last update, as burial;\nthe fraction of the interval it spent under water;\nrainfall and evapotranspiration from veg_water_file;\nthe day of the year from refdate.\nPlants spread. Bare cells are colonised from occupied neighbours at about 0.4 m per year (Keijsers et al. 2015), with occasional jumps (Bonte et al. 2021).\nRoots resist waves. When a storm cuts back the dune, sand in the rooted layer costs more of the waves’ work to remove than bare sand (Figlus et al. 2014; De Battisti and Griffin 2020).\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing Plots\nusing Printf\nusing Random","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/14_marram_foredune/#The-beach-and-its-weather","page":"A foredune built by marram grass","text":"A transect rises from below the low-tide line to a backshore at 3 m, 100 m inland. Beyond 126 m the hinterland is fixed, so the bare sand behind the dune does not blow away. There is a semidiurnal tide of 1 m. Every day has a new onshore wind: a Weibull speed with a mean of 7 m/s, from directions within 40° of shore-normal. Rain falls on 45 % of days, and potential evapotranspiration follows the seasons. The weather is drawn from a seeded random number generator, so the vignette is reproducible.\n\nday = 86400.0\nyears = 3\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\nx = collect(0.0:1.0:150.0)\nzb0 = @. min(-1.0 + x / 25, 3.0)\nnd = 365 * years + 1\ntd = (0:(nd - 1)) .* day\nwind = hcat(td, [7.0 / 0.886 * sqrt(-log(rand(rng))) for _ in td],\n            [270.0 + 80 * (rand(rng) - 0.5) for _ in td])\ntt = collect(0.0:3600.0:(nd * day))\ntide = hcat(tt, sin.(2pi .* tt ./ (12.42 * 3600)))\ndoy = [(k % 365) + 1 for k in 0:(nd - 1)]\nwater = hcat(td, [rand(rng) < 0.45 ? -4.9 * log(rand(rng)) : 0.0 for _ in td],\n             @. 2.0 - 1.5 * cos(2pi * (doy - 15) / 365))\nbase = (; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=zb0, ne_file=(@. ifelse(x >= 126, zb0, -10.0)),\n        th_nelayer=true, wind_file=wind, tide_file=tide, process_tide=true,\n        grain_size=0.0003, dt=3600.0, tstop=years * 365day, output_times=30day,\n        refdate=\"2020-01-01 00:00\", process_avalanche=true,\n        output_vars=[:zb, :veg_N, :hveg, :rhoveg, :veg_P, :veg_Gb, :veg_zdot])\nnothing\n\nMarram is planted between 110 and 125 m at 30 % of the density of a closed stand, 141 shoots per m². The same beach is also run without plants.\n\nplanting = @. ifelse(110 <= x <= 125, 0.3, 0.0)\nmarram = solve(AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; base..., process_vegetation=true,\n                                              veg_model=:marram, veg_file=planting,\n                                              veg_water_file=water)); rng=Xoshiro(2))\nbare = solve(AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; base...)))\nnothing","title":"The beach and its weather"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/14_marram_foredune/#The-dune","page":"A foredune built by marram grass","text":"z = transect(marram, :zb)\nzbare = transect(bare, :zb)\nvolume(zz) = sum(max.(zz[101:end] .- 3.0, 0.0))     # m³ per m of beach, above 3 m\nplt = plot(x, zbare[:, end]; color=:gray, lw=2, label=\"bare, year 3\",\n           xlabel=\"distance (m)\", ylabel=\"bed level (m)\", xlims=(80, 150), legend=:topleft)\nfor (k, c) in zip((13, 25, 37), (:lightgreen, :green, :darkgreen))\n    plot!(plt, x, z[:, k]; lw=2, color=c, label=\"marram, year $((k - 1) ÷ 12)\")\nend\nplot!(plt, x, zb0; color=:black, ls=:dash, label=\"initial\")\nplt\n\n(Image: )\n\nfor k in (13, 25, 37)\n    @printf(\"year %d: dune volume %.1f m³/m with marram, %.1f m³/m bare; crest %.2f m\\n\",\n            (k - 1) ÷ 12, volume(z[:, k]), volume(zbare[:, k]), maximum(z[:, k]))\nend\n\nyear 1: dune volume 20.1 m³/m with marram, 0.3 m³/m bare; crest 4.81 m\nyear 2: dune volume 35.7 m³/m with marram, 0.3 m³/m bare; crest 5.78 m\nyear 3: dune volume 53.6 m³/m with marram, 0.3 m³/m bare; crest 6.80 m\n\nThe bare beach builds no dune: sand blown off the beach crosses the flat backshore. The planted strip slows the wind at the bed, traps the sand and grows a dune several metres high in three years. The wind blows onshore every day here, so this rate is high for a real coast.","title":"The dune"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/14_marram_foredune/#The-plants","page":"A foredune built by marram grass","text":"N = transect(marram, :veg_N)\nmonths = (0:(size(N, 2) - 1))\nheatmap(months, x[95:135], N[95:135, :]; xlabel=\"months\", ylabel=\"distance (m)\",\n        colorbar_title=\"shoots per m²\", c=:Greens)\n\n(Image: )\n\nShoot density rises each summer and falls back each winter.\n\nSeaward edge: the stand spreads over the bare beach a little each year, which is as much as the front speed allows.\nCrest: the stand thins where the dune grows fastest.\n\nThe diagnostics at the end of the run show why:\n\nG = transect(marram, :veg_Gb)\nzdot = transect(marram, :veg_zdot)\ntr = MarramTraits()\nfor i in 106:4:126\n    @printf(\"x = %3d m: bed %.2f m, burial %5.2f m/season, G_b %.2f, shoots %3.0f per m²\\n\",\n            x[i], z[i, end], zdot[i, end] * Aeolis.season_length(tr), G[i, end], N[i, end])\nend\n\nx = 105 m: bed 3.03 m, burial  0.47 m/season, G_b 0.82, shoots   0 per m²\nx = 109 m: bed 5.62 m, burial  0.50 m/season, G_b 0.77, shoots 199 per m²\nx = 113 m: bed 6.60 m, burial  0.72 m/season, G_b 0.30, shoots  30 per m²\nx = 117 m: bed 6.12 m, burial  0.50 m/season, G_b 0.77, shoots 126 per m²\nx = 121 m: bed 5.73 m, burial  0.34 m/season, G_b 0.99, shoots 259 per m²\nx = 125 m: bed 3.95 m, burial -0.04 m/season, G_b 0.98, shoots 414 per m²\n\nWhere the crest is buried by about 0.7 m per growing season, more than twice the optimum (Nolet et al. 2018), the burial multiplier G_b falls to about 0.3. Fewer shoots survive there than on the flanks. The landward edge receives little sand. The model would slowly lower vigour there through soil-biota pressure, but three years is too short for that to show.","title":"The plants"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/14_marram_foredune/#How-densely-to-plant","page":"A foredune built by marram grass","text":"The stand is planted at densities from 2 % to 100 % of a closed stand, and run for two years:\n\nfor c in (0.02, 0.1, 1.0)\n    p = AeolisParameters(; base..., tstop=2 * 365day, output_times=365day,\n                         process_vegetation=true, veg_model=:marram,\n                         veg_file=(@. ifelse(110 <= x <= 125, c, 0.0)),\n                         veg_water_file=water)\n    zz = transect(solve(AeolisProblem(p); rng=Xoshiro(2)), :zb)\n    @printf(\"planted at %4.0f %%: dune volume %.1f m³/m after two years\\n\", 100c,\n            volume(zz[:, end]))\nend\n\nplanted at    2 %: dune volume 33.1 m³/m after two years\nplanted at   10 %: dune volume 34.4 m³/m after two years\nplanted at  100 %: dune volume 38.0 m³/m after two years\n\nEven a sparse planting builds most of the dune, for two reasons:\n\nShelter saturates. The drag on full-height marram reaches its cap at about 45 shoots per m².\nSparse stands fill in. They grow toward their capacity within a season or two.\n\nBonte et al. (2021) found that dunes grow fastest at intermediate cover. The model does not reproduce this, either here or in two-dimensional runs with patchy planting, where dune volume also rose with the area planted. Why the two differ is not yet understood. The supply of sand, the measure of dune growth, and how trapping depends on cover in their cellular model are candidates.","title":"How densely to plant"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/14_marram_foredune/#A-storm","page":"A foredune built by marram grass","text":"After the three years, a storm attacks the dune for two days. A surge of 2.5 m and waves 3 m high with a 10 s period collide with the dune face, which erodes as in Palmsten and Holman (2012).\n\nThe storm run starts from the state after three years (u0), so the roots are those the dune grew. It is repeated with the roots made ineffective: a traits file sets the strength of bare sand, tau0, so high that reinforcement adds nothing. The light wind moves no sand.\n\nThe dune toe is set at 2.7 m. AeoLiS takes as the toe the point of the profile nearest that elevation, and the hinterland lies at exactly 3 m.\n\nt0 = years * 365day\nstorm = (; tstart=t0, tstop=t0 + 2day, output_times=2day,\n         wind_file=[0.0 2.0 270.0; 4000day 2.0 270.0], tide_file=[0.0 2.5; 4000day 2.5],\n         wave_file=[0.0 3.0 10.0; 4000day 3.0 10.0], process_wave=true, process_runup=true,\n         process_dune_erosion=true, dune_toe_elevation=2.7, beach_slope=0.04)\ninert = tempname() * \".toml\"\nwrite(inert, \"tau0 = 1e12\\n\")\nu3 = marram.u\neroded = Dict{String,Float64}()\nplt = plot(x, u3.zb[:, 2]; color=:black, lw=2, label=\"before the storm\",\n           xlabel=\"distance (m)\", ylabel=\"bed level (m)\", xlims=(90, 135), legend=:topleft)\nfor (label, kw, c) in ((\"with roots\", (;), :darkgreen), (\"roots ineffective\", (; veg_traits_file=inert), :orange))\n    p = AeolisParameters(; base..., storm..., process_vegetation=true, veg_model=:marram,\n                         veg_file=planting, kw...)\n    z1 = solve(AeolisProblem(p; u0=u3); rng=Xoshiro(3)).u.zb[:, 2]\n    eroded[label] = sum(max.(u3.zb[:, 2] .- z1, 0.0))\n    plot!(plt, x, z1; lw=2, color=c, label=label)\nend\n@printf(\"eroded: %.1f m³/m with roots, %.1f m³/m without; %.0f %% less\\n\",\n        eroded[\"with roots\"], eroded[\"roots ineffective\"],\n        100 * (1 - eroded[\"with roots\"] / eroded[\"roots ineffective\"]))\nplt\n\neroded: 29.7 m³/m with roots, 42.6 m³/m without; 30 % less\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe roots keep a third of the dune face that the storm would otherwise take. Flume experiments found 8-37 % less erosion with vegetation (Figlus et al. 2014; Kosmalla et al. 2026), and intact cores of marram 36 % (De Battisti and Griffin 2020). The fine-root fraction of the traits was fitted to these, and the run falls in their range.\n\nThe model does not represent one effect seen in a large flume (Feagin et al. 2023): a vegetated dune that eroded faster, because plants changed the run-up and infiltration of the swash.","title":"A storm"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/14_marram_foredune/#What-remains-uncertain","page":"A foredune built by marram grass","text":"The burial response and soil-biota pressure rest on few studies.\nRoot reinforcement: the tensile strength of marram roots has not been measured, and the erosion data constrain only its product with the fine-root fraction.\n\nBoth uncertainties are listed with ranges in marram_info.\n\n<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Bonte2021\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nBonte, Dries, Femke Batsleer, Sam Provoost, et al. 2021. “Biomorphogenic Feedbacks and the Spatial Organization of a Dominant Grass Steer Dune Development.” Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution 9: 761336. https://doi.org/10.3389/fevo.2021.761336.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-DeBattisti2020\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nDe Battisti, Davide, and John N. Griffin. 2020. “Below-Ground Biomass of Plants, with a Key Contribution of Buried Shoots, Increases Foredune Resistance to Wave Swash.” Annals of Botany 125: 325–34. https://doi.org/10.1093/aob/mcz125.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Feagin2023\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nFeagin, Rusty A., Rachel A. Innocenti, Hailey Bond, et al. 2023. “Does Vegetation Accelerate Coastal Dune Erosion During Extreme Events?” Science Advances 9: eadg7135. https://doi.org/10.1126/sciadv.adg7135.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Figlus2014\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nFiglus, Jens, Jacob M. Sigren, Anna R. Armitage, and Robert C. Tyler.\n\n“Erosion of Vegetated Coastal Dunes.” *Coastal Engineering\n\nProceedings* 1 (34): sediment.20. https://doi.org/10.9753/icce.v34.sediment.20.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Keijsers2015\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nKeijsers, Joep G. S., Alma V. De Groot, and Michel J. P. M. Riksen.\n\n“Vegetation and Sedimentation on Coastal Foredunes.”\n\nGeomorphology 228: 723–34. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.geomorph.2014.10.027.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Kosmalla2026\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nKosmalla, Viktoria, Oliver Lojek, Lukas Ahrenbeck, et al. 2026. “Vegetation Effects on Dune Erosion Under Wave Collision: Influence of Planting Density, Biomass Distribution and Arrangement in Scaled Experiments.” Coastal Engineering 204: 104899. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coastaleng.2025.104899.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-LaporteFauret2024\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nLaporte-Fauret, Quentin, Meagan Wengrove, Peter Ruggiero, et al. 2024. “A New Approach to Account for Species-Specific Sand Capture by Plants in an Aeolian Sediment Transport and Coastal Dune Building Model.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface 129: e2024JF007867. https://doi.org/10.1029/2024JF007867.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Nolet2018\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nNolet, Corjan, Marinka van Puijenbroek, Juha Suomalainen, Juul Limpens, and Michel Riksen. 2018. “UAV-Imaging to Model Growth Response of Marram Grass to Sand Burial: Implications for Coastal Dune Development.” Aeolian Research 31: 50–61. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aeolia.2017.08.006.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Palmsten2012\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nPalmsten, Margaret L., and Robert A. Holman. 2012. “Laboratory Investigation of Dune Erosion Using Stereo Video.” Coastal Engineering 60: 123–35. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coastaleng.2011.09.003.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Walter2012\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nWalter, Benjamin, Christof Gromke, and Michael Lehning. 2012. “Shear-Stress Partitioning in Live Plant Canopies and Modifications to Raupach’s Model.” Boundary-Layer Meteorology 144: 217–41. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10546-012-9719-4.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"What remains uncertain"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/15_marram_uncertainty/#Uncertainty-in-the-marram-grass-model","page":"Uncertainty in the marram grass model","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nThree predictions for one cell\nWhich priors matter\nHistory matching\nPredictions with ranges","title":"Uncertainty in the marram grass model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/15_marram_uncertainty/#Overview","page":"Uncertainty in the marram grass model","text":"Every trait of the marram grass model has an evidence grade (marram_info):\n\nM traits were measured on marram;\nD traits were fitted to experiments (vignette 13);\nP traits are priors, with no direct measurement.\n\nThis vignette asks how much the priors matter, in three steps: 1. a Sobol analysis over the priors, which shows which unknowns drive which predictions; 2. history matching, which keeps only the prior draws that still reproduce the calibration experiments; 3. predictions, with ranges, from the trait sets that remain: first for one cell, then for the foredune and storm of vignette 14.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing Dates\nusing Plots\nusing Printf\nusing QuasiMonteCarlo\nusing Random\nusing Statistics\nconst AE = Aeolis","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/15_marram_uncertainty/#Three-predictions-for-one-cell","page":"Uncertainty in the marram grass model","text":"A stand at half its maximum density goes through three phases of sand supply:\n\nyears 0-4: 0.3 m of sand per growing season, about the optimum;\nyears 4-7: none;\nyear 7 onward: 0.3 m per season again.\n\nThree predictions are read from this run:\n\ndecline: the shoot density after three years without sand, relative to that at year 4;\nrecovery: the shoot density a year after burial resumes, relative to that at year 4;\nerosion reduction: how much the roots of the year-4 stand reduce wave erosion of the dune, c_r / (tau0 + c_r).\n\nfunction predictions(tr)\n    z(t) = (4 * 365 <= t < 7 * 365) ? 0.0 : 0.3 / AE.season_length(tr)\n    N0 = 0.5 * tr.N_max\n    cell = MarramCell(; N=N0, h=0.5, Br=AE.root_target(N0, 0.3, tr), zr=0.5, s=tr.s_fc)\n    sol = simulate_marram(cell, (t, d) -> (; dzb=z(t), s=tr.s_fc), tr; days=8 * 365,\n                          start=Date(2020, 1, 1))\n    i4, i7, i8 = 4 * 365 + 1, 7 * 365 + 1, 8 * 365 + 1\n    return [sol.N[i7] / sol.N[i4], sol.N[i8] / sol.N[i4], sol.cr[i4] / (tr.tau0 + sol.cr[i4])]\nend\nlabels = [\"decline\", \"recovery\", \"erosion reduction\"]\nround.(predictions(MarramTraits()); digits=3)\n\n3-element Vector{Float64}:\n 0.739\n 0.832\n 0.338","title":"Three predictions for one cell"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/15_marram_uncertainty/#Which-priors-matter","page":"Uncertainty in the marram grass model","text":"The Sobol indices of a prediction split its variance among the traits:\n\nthe first-order index S1 is the share that a trait causes alone;\nthe total index ST adds its interactions with the other traits.\n\nThey are computed here with the estimators of Saltelli et al. (2010) (first order) and Jansen (1999) (total). The design is one scrambled Sobol sequence in twice as many dimensions as there are traits, split into the two matrices the estimators need.\n\nfunction sobol_indices(f, lb, ub, n)\n    k = length(lb)\n    X = QuasiMonteCarlo.sample(n, vcat(lb, lb), vcat(ub, ub),\n                               SobolSample(; R=OwenScramble(; base=2, pad=32)))\n    A, B = X[1:k, :], X[(k + 1):end, :]\n    fA = reduce(hcat, [f(A[:, j]) for j in 1:n])\n    fB = reduce(hcat, [f(B[:, j]) for j in 1:n])\n    V = vec(var(hcat(fA, fB); dims=2))\n    S1 = zeros(size(fA, 1), k)\n    ST = zeros(size(fA, 1), k)\n    for i in 1:k\n        ABi = copy(A)\n        ABi[i, :] .= B[i, :]\n        fABi = reduce(hcat, [f(ABi[:, j]) for j in 1:n])\n        S1[:, i] .= vec(mean(fB .* (fABi .- fA); dims=2)) ./ V\n        ST[:, i] .= vec(mean((fA .- fABi) .^ 2; dims=2)) ./ 2 ./ V\n    end\n    return (; S1, ST)\nend\nnothing\n\nAs a check, on the Ishigami function the indices are known exactly: S1 = 0.314, 0.442 and 0, and ST = 0.558, 0.442 and 0.244.\n\nRandom.seed!(1)\nish(x) = [sin(x[1]) + 7 * sin(x[2])^2 + 0.1 * x[3]^4 * sin(x[1])]\ncheck = sobol_indices(ish, fill(-float(pi), 3), fill(float(pi), 3), 4096)\nprintln(\"S1 \", round.(check.S1[1, :]; digits=3), \", ST \", round.(check.ST[1, :]; digits=3))\n\nS1 [0.306, 0.443, -0.005], ST [0.557, 0.442, 0.244]\n\nThe analysis covers the priors that act in a single cell. Colonisation and drag act only on a grid, and the ensemble below covers them.\n\ngrid_only = (:f_jump, :jump_distance, :N_new, :h_new, :zr_new, :establishment, :kappa)\npriors = filter(r -> !(r.name in grid_only), marram_ranges())\nnames = [r.name for r in priors]\nbase = MarramTraits()\nRandom.seed!(2)\nsobol = sobol_indices(x -> predictions(MarramTraits(base, names, x)),\n                      [r.low for r in priors], [r.high for r in priors], 2048)\nplots = map(1:3) do k\n    order = sortperm(sobol.ST[k, :]; rev=true)[1:5]\n    pos = 1:5\n    p = bar(pos .- 0.2, max.(sobol.S1[k, order], 0); bar_width=0.4, label=\"S1\",\n            title=labels[k], ylims=(0, 1.05), legend=k == 1)\n    bar!(p, pos .+ 0.2, sobol.ST[k, order]; bar_width=0.4, label=\"ST\",\n         xticks=(pos, string.(names[order])), xrotation=30)\nend\nplot(plots...; layout=(1, 3), size=(900, 320), bottom_margin=8Plots.mm)\n\n(Image: )\n\nfor k in 1:3\n    order = sortperm(sobol.ST[k, :]; rev=true)[1:3]\n    @printf(\"%-18s %s\\n\", labels[k],\n            join([@sprintf(\"%s %.2f\", names[j], sobol.ST[k, j]) for j in order], \", \"))\nend\n\ndecline            P_seed 0.63, p_max 0.39, burial_window 0.00\nrecovery           p_max 0.44, tau_relief 0.35, P_seed 0.22\nerosion reduction  T_r 1.00, P_seed 0.00, p_max 0.00\n\nEach prediction is driven by one or two unknowns:\n\ndecline by the seed of the soil-biota build-up, P_seed, and the strength of the pressure, p_max;\nrecovery by those two and by how fast fresh sand relieves pressure, tau_relief;\nerosion reduction almost entirely by the tensile strength of marram roots, T_r, which has never been measured.\n\nThese are the measurements that would most improve the model.","title":"Which priors matter"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/15_marram_uncertainty/#History-matching","page":"Uncertainty in the marram grass model","text":"Some D traits were fitted with the priors held at their defaults. For example, the time scale of soil-biota pressure tau_P was fitted with P_seed and p_max at theirs. Drawing the priors independently can therefore give trait sets that no longer reproduce the experiments.\n\nSo the priors are drawn 3000 times, together with the three D traits fitted with them: tau_P, d_f and the fine-root fraction f_fine. A draw is kept only if it still reproduces three experiments:\n\nthe shoots of the mesocosm of Reijers et al. (2021), within 15 % in all three treatments;\nthe burial response of Nolet et al. (2018) below the optimum, with a root-mean-square difference below 0.05;\nthe erosion reduction of a mature stand, between 26 and 41 %. That is the 31-36 % of marram in flume and core experiments (Kosmalla et al. 2026; De Battisti and Griffin 2020), widened by 5 points.\n\nfunction plausible(tr)\n    e = marram_experiment(:reijers2021, tr)\n    all(abs.(e.shoots ./ e.shoots_observed .- 1) .< 0.15) || return false\n    n = marram_experiment(:nolet2018, tr)\n    below = n.burial .<= tr.burial_opt\n    ndvi = vec(mean(n.ndvi_observed; dims=2))\n    sqrt(mean(abs2, n.response[below] .- ndvi[below])) < 0.05 || return false\n    return 0.26 <= marram_experiment(:root_erosion, tr).stand_reduction <= 0.41\nend\ndraws = marram_sample(Xoshiro(7), 3000; add=(:tau_P, :d_f, :f_fine))\nkept = filter(plausible, draws)\n@printf(\"%d of %d draws reproduce the experiments\\n\", length(kept), length(draws))\n\n54 of 3000 draws reproduce the experiments\n\nAbout 2 % of the draws survive, because the calibration data tie the traits together. The kept draws show how:\n\np1 = scatter([d.P_seed for d in draws], [d.tau_P for d in draws]; ms=2, color=:lightgray,\n             label=\"prior draws\", xlabel=\"P_seed\", ylabel=\"tau_P (years)\")\nscatter!(p1, [d.P_seed for d in kept], [d.tau_P for d in kept]; ms=4, color=:darkgreen,\n         label=\"reproduce the experiments\")\np2 = scatter([d.T_r for d in draws], [d.f_fine for d in draws]; ms=2, color=:lightgray,\n             legend=false, xlabel=\"T_r (MPa)\", ylabel=\"f_fine\")\nscatter!(p2, [d.T_r for d in kept], [d.f_fine for d in kept]; ms=4, color=:darkgreen)\nplot(p1, p2; layout=(1, 2), size=(850, 350), bottom_margin=5Plots.mm, left_margin=5Plots.mm)\n\n(Image: )\n\ncorr(a, b) = cor([getfield(d, a) for d in kept], [getfield(d, b) for d in kept])\n@printf(\"correlation among kept draws: P_seed and tau_P %.2f, T_r and f_fine %.2f\\n\",\n        corr(:P_seed, :tau_P), corr(:T_r, :f_fine))\n\ncorrelation among kept draws: P_seed and tau_P 0.66, T_r and f_fine -0.82\n\nTwo relationships stand out:\n\nA larger seed of soil biota needs a slower build-up (tau_P). This keeps the mesocosm, on fresh sand, from declining in its first year.\nStronger roots need fewer fine roots. The erosion experiments fix only the product of tensile strength T_r and the fine-root fraction f_fine, not each one.","title":"History matching"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/15_marram_uncertainty/#Predictions-with-ranges","page":"Uncertainty in the marram grass model","text":"For one cell, the three predictions under the prior and under the kept trait sets:\n\nq(v) = quantile(v, [0.05, 0.5, 0.95])\nprior_pred = reduce(hcat, predictions.(draws[1:500]))\nkept_pred = reduce(hcat, predictions.(kept))\nfor k in 1:3\n    a, b = q(prior_pred[k, :]), q(kept_pred[k, :])\n    @printf(\"%-18s prior %.2f (%.2f-%.2f); kept %.2f (%.2f-%.2f)\\n\", labels[k], a[2], a[1],\n            a[3], b[2], b[1], b[3])\nend\n\ndecline            prior 0.73 (0.38-0.95); kept 0.70 (0.62-0.81)\nrecovery           prior 0.74 (0.47-0.93); kept 0.70 (0.56-0.85)\nerosion reduction  prior 0.47 (0.28-0.65); kept 0.36 (0.30-0.41)\n\nThe numbers are medians with 5-95 % ranges. The experiments narrow all three predictions:\n\ndecline and recovery, through the soil-biota traits;\nthe erosion reduction, through the product of root strength and fine-root fraction.\n\nFor the foredune, 32 of the kept trait sets are run through two years of the vignette 14 beach and then its storm, as an EnsembleProblem. Each member reads its traits from its own file (write_marram_traits).\n\nday = 86400.0\nyears = 2\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\nx = collect(0.0:1.0:150.0)\nzb0 = @. min(-1.0 + x / 25, 3.0)\nnd = 365 * years + 1\ntd = (0:(nd - 1)) .* day\nwind = hcat(td, [7.0 / 0.886 * sqrt(-log(rand(rng))) for _ in td],\n            [270.0 + 80 * (rand(rng) - 0.5) for _ in td])\ntt = collect(0.0:3600.0:(nd * day))\ntide = hcat(tt, sin.(2pi .* tt ./ (12.42 * 3600)))\ndoy = [(k % 365) + 1 for k in 0:(nd - 1)]\nwater = hcat(td, [rand(rng) < 0.45 ? -4.9 * log(rand(rng)) : 0.0 for _ in td],\n             @. 2.0 - 1.5 * cos(2pi * (doy - 15) / 365))\nbase_run = (; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=zb0, ne_file=(@. ifelse(x >= 126, zb0, -10.0)),\n            th_nelayer=true, wind_file=wind, tide_file=tide, process_tide=true,\n            grain_size=0.0003, dt=3600.0, tstop=years * 365day, output_times=365day,\n            refdate=\"2020-01-01 00:00\", process_avalanche=true, output_vars=[:zb],\n            process_vegetation=true, veg_model=:marram,\n            veg_file=(@. ifelse(110 <= x <= 125, 0.3, 0.0)), veg_water_file=water)\ndir = mktempdir()\nmembers = 32\nfiles = [write_marram_traits(joinpath(dir, \"member$i.toml\"), kept[i]) for i in 1:members]\ninert = [write_marram_traits(joinpath(dir, \"inert$i.toml\"), MarramTraits(kept[i]; tau0=1e12))\n         for i in 1:members]\ngrow = EnsembleProblem(AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; base_run...));\n                       prob_func=(pr, ctx) -> remake(pr; veg_traits_file=files[ctx.sim_id]))\ngrown = solve(grow, EulerBackward(), AE.SciMLBase.EnsembleThreads(); trajectories=members)\nt0 = years * 365day\nstorm = (; tstart=t0, tstop=t0 + 2day, output_times=2day,\n         wind_file=[0.0 2.0 270.0; 4000day 2.0 270.0], tide_file=[0.0 2.5; 4000day 2.5],\n         wave_file=[0.0 3.0 10.0; 4000day 3.0 10.0], process_wave=true, process_runup=true,\n         process_dune_erosion=true, dune_toe_elevation=2.7, beach_slope=0.04)\nstorm_prob = AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; base_run..., storm...))\nfunction eroded(traitfiles)\n    ep = EnsembleProblem(storm_prob;\n                         prob_func=(pr, ctx) -> remake(pr; u0=grown.u[ctx.sim_id].u,\n                                                       veg_traits_file=traitfiles[ctx.sim_id]))\n    es = solve(ep, EulerBackward(), AE.SciMLBase.EnsembleThreads(); trajectories=members)\n    return [sum(max.(grown.u[i].u.zb[:, 2] .- es.u[i].u.zb[:, 2], 0.0)) for i in 1:members]\nend\nwith_roots = eroded(files)\nwithout_roots = eroded(inert)\nvolume = [sum(max.(s.u.zb[101:end, 2] .- 3.0, 0.0)) for s in grown.u]\nfor (lab, v) in ((\"dune volume after 2 years (m³/m)\", volume),\n                 (\"storm erosion with roots (m³/m)\", with_roots),\n                 (\"storm erosion without roots (m³/m)\", without_roots),\n                 (\"reduction by roots\", 1 .- with_roots ./ without_roots))\n    a = q(v)\n    @printf(\"%-36s %.2f (%.2f-%.2f)\\n\", lab, a[2], a[1], a[3])\nend\n\ndune volume after 2 years (m³/m)     37.80 (34.82-42.06)\nstorm erosion with roots (m³/m)      27.16 (24.08-30.39)\nstorm erosion without roots (m³/m)   35.66 (32.84-39.96)\nreduction by roots                   0.25 (0.14-0.32)\n\nThese ranges are the uncertainty left by the priors once the calibration experiments are respected. They do not include:\n\nuncertainty in the M and D traits themselves;\nuncertainty in the forcing, a single synthetic weather series here;\nuncertainty in AeoLiS’s own transport and erosion parameters.\n\nThe analysis also points to the measurements that would narrow the ranges most:\n\nhow fast soil biota build up and how strongly they act (P_seed, p_max);\nhow fast fresh sand relieves them (tau_relief);\nthe tensile strength and fine-root fraction of marram roots, which the erosion data cannot separate.\n\n<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-DeBattisti2020\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nDe Battisti, Davide, and John N. Griffin. 2020. “Below-Ground Biomass of Plants, with a Key Contribution of Buried Shoots, Increases Foredune Resistance to Wave Swash.” Annals of Botany 125: 325–34. https://doi.org/10.1093/aob/mcz125.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Jansen1999\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nJansen, Michiel J. W. 1999. “Analysis of Variance Designs for Model Output.” Computer Physics Communications 117: 35–43. https://doi.org/10.1016/S0010-4655(98)00154-4.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Kosmalla2026\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nKosmalla, Viktoria, Oliver Lojek, Lukas Ahrenbeck, et al. 2026. “Vegetation Effects on Dune Erosion Under Wave Collision: Influence of Planting Density, Biomass Distribution and Arrangement in Scaled Experiments.” Coastal Engineering 204: 104899. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coastaleng.2025.104899.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Nolet2018\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nNolet, Corjan, Marinka van Puijenbroek, Juha Suomalainen, Juul Limpens, and Michel Riksen. 2018. “UAV-Imaging to Model Growth Response of Marram Grass to Sand Burial: Implications for Coastal Dune Development.” Aeolian Research 31: 50–61. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aeolia.2017.08.006.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Reijers2021\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nReijers, Valérie C., Selwyn Hoeks, Jim van Belzen, et al. 2021. “Sediment Availability Provokes a Shift from Brownian to L<span class=\"nocase\">évy</span>-Like Clonal Expansion in a Dune Building Grass.” Ecology Letters 24: 258–68. https://doi.org/10.1111/ele.13638.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Saltelli2010\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nSaltelli, Andrea, Paola Annoni, Ivano Azzini, Francesca Campolongo, Marco Ratto, and Stefano Tarantola. 2010. “Variance Based Sensitivity Analysis of Model Output. Design and Estimator for the Total Sensitivity Index.” Computer Physics Communications 181: 259–70. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpc.2009.09.018.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"Predictions with ranges"},{"category":"section","location":"api_marram/#Marram-grass","page":"Marram grass","text":"A model of marram grass (Calamagrostis arenaria, formerly Ammophila arenaria) in one grid cell. Its state is a MarramCell:\n\nshoot density and canopy height;\nroot and rhizome mass, and the thickness of the rooted sand layer;\nroot-zone soil moisture;\nsoil-biota pressure;\nthe current spells of submergence and drought.\n\nEach process is a scalar function of the MarramTraits, with its source and evidence grade in its docstring. marram_rates collects every multiplier and mortality, marram_step advances a cell, and simulate_marram runs one cell under prescribed burial, water and flooding.\n\nmarram_experiment re-runs the published experiments to which the traits were calibrated, next to the published numbers.\n\nOn the grid, veg_model = marram (with process_vegetation) makes every cell a marram cell; the default veg_model = legacy is AeoLiS's vegetation. The grid model is set up by Aeolis.initialize_marram!:\n\neach time step, Aeolis.marram_cover! gives the shear reduction its cover;\nevery veg_interval, Aeolis.marram_update! advances the cells and Aeolis.marram_colonise! colonises bare ones;\nwith process_dune_erosion, roots resist the cut-back of Aeolis.dune_erosion!: sand in a cell's rooted layer costs 1 + veg_cr / tau0 times as much of the waves' work as bare sand.\n\nThe grid model's parameters are:\n\nParameter Default Meaning\nveg_model legacy marram for this model\nveg_file none initial shoot density, as a fraction of N_max\nveg_traits_file none TOML file of traits (see marram_traits)\nveg_water_file none time (s), rainfall and potential evapotranspiration (mm per day); without it, roots never lack water\nveg_interval 86400 s interval between updates, in accelerated time\nveg_random true random colonisation; false colonises at the expected time\n\ngamma_vegshear, vegshear_type, veg_sigma and veg_min_elevation act as for the legacy model. The veg_* state variables hold the cells and their diagnostics, and can be saved like any other.","title":"Marram grass"},{"category":"section","location":"api_marram/#Traits","page":"Marram grass","text":"Evidence is M (measured on marram, or on A. breviligulata where the source says so), D (derived from measurements) or P (a prior without a direct measurement). The range bounds the values for sensitivity analysis.\n\nusing Aeolis, Markdown\nrows = [\"| `$(r.name)` | $(r.default) | $(r.unit) | $(r.evidence) | $(r.range[1])-$(r.range[2]) | $(isempty(r.source) ? \"-\" : r.source) | $(r.description) |\"\n        for r in marram_info()]\nMarkdown.parse(join([\"| Trait | Default | Unit | Evidence | Range | Source | Description |\",\n                     \"|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\", rows...], \"\\n\"))\n          Trait Default               Unit Evidence          Range                Source                                                                 Description\n  ––––––––––––– ––––––– –––––––––––––––––– –––––––– –––––––––––––– ––––––––––––––––––––– –––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––––\n   \u001b[36mseason_start\u001b[39m    91.0        day of year        M     60.0-121.0             Nolet2018                                   First day of the growing season (1 April)\n     \u001b[36mseason_end\u001b[39m   273.0        day of year        M    213.0-304.0             Nolet2018                               Last day of the growing season (30 September)\n    \u001b[36mseason_ramp\u001b[39m    14.0                day        P       0.0-30.0             Bonte2021                        Length of the linear ramps at each end of the season\n  \u001b[36mburial_window\u001b[39m    30.0                day        P      14.0-90.0             Nolet2018            Time scale of the moving average of the rate of bed-level change\n     \u001b[36mburial_opt\u001b[39m    0.31           m/season        M      0.27-0.35             Nolet2018                        Burial per growing season at which growth is fastest\n    \u001b[36mburial_c_lo\u001b[39m     2.0           m/season        D        1.0-5.0           Reijers2021                              Width of the burial response below the optimum\n    \u001b[36mburial_c_hi\u001b[39m    0.26           m/season        M      0.08-0.47             Nolet2018                              Width of the burial response above the optimum\n              \u001b[36mr\u001b[39m     6.3             1/year        D        4.0-8.0           Reijers2021       Relative growth rate of shoot density per year of full growing season\n          \u001b[36mN_max\u001b[39m   470.0              1/m^2        M   250.0-1000.0          Kosmalla2025                                             Shoot density of a closed stand\n          \u001b[36mN_min\u001b[39m     0.5              1/m^2        P        0.1-2.0                     -                                    Shoot density below which a cell is bare\n             \u001b[36mm0\u001b[39m     0.2             1/year        D        0.1-0.4          Kosmalla2025                                                  Background shoot mortality\n          \u001b[36mh_min\u001b[39m     0.1                  m        P       0.05-0.2      MaunLapierre1984               Exposed canopy height below which buried shoots are smothered\n         \u001b[36mtau_sm\u001b[39m    56.0                day        D     30.0-120.0           Reijers2021                               Time scale of death of fully smothered shoots\n          \u001b[36mh_max\u001b[39m     0.8                  m        M       0.65-1.0          Kosmalla2025                                                   Height of a mature canopy\n             \u001b[36me0\u001b[39m   0.003              m/day        D    0.002-0.004           Reijers2021                                             Shoot elongation without burial\n             \u001b[36me1\u001b[39m   0.011 (m/day)/(m/season)        D    0.008-0.014           Reijers2021                                    Increase of shoot elongation with burial\n       \u001b[36mporosity\u001b[39m    0.42                  -        M      0.35-0.45         Homberger2026                                                   Porosity of the root zone\n            \u001b[36mZ_r\u001b[39m     0.5                  m        M        0.3-1.0         Homberger2026                                                      Depth of the root zone\n            \u001b[36ms_h\u001b[39m    0.01                  -        M    0.005-0.024         Homberger2026                             Relative soil moisture at the hygroscopic point\n            \u001b[36ms_w\u001b[39m   0.096                  -        M    0.024-0.114         Homberger2024                                 Relative soil moisture at wilting of marram\n         \u001b[36ms_star\u001b[39m   0.145                  -        M     0.12-0.155         Homberger2026                Relative soil moisture below which marram closes its stomata\n           \u001b[36ms_fc\u001b[39m    0.16                  -        M       0.08-0.2         Homberger2026                                    Relative soil moisture at field capacity\n            \u001b[36mE_w\u001b[39m     0.1             mm/day        M       0.05-0.2         Homberger2026                                   Evaporation from the root zone at wilting\n          \u001b[36mE_max\u001b[39m     5.0             mm/day        M       2.3-19.7         Homberger2026                       Transpiration of a closed canopy without water stress\n            \u001b[36mK_s\u001b[39m  5040.0             mm/day        M 1000.0-10000.0         Homberger2026                                            Saturated hydraulic conductivity\n         \u001b[36mb_leak\u001b[39m    4.05                  -        M      4.05-12.1         Homberger2026                                              Exponent of the drainage curve\n   \u001b[36minterception\u001b[39m     0.5             mm/day        M        0.0-1.0         Homberger2026                                          Rainfall intercepted by the canopy\n       \u001b[36mq_stress\u001b[39m     3.0                  -        M        1.0-4.0         Homberger2026                                          Exponent of the water stress curve\n          \u001b[36mt_dry\u001b[39m    21.0                day        D      14.0-28.0             Dixon2004                        Spell below wilting after which drought kills shoots\n          \u001b[36mk_dry\u001b[39m    0.03              1/day        D      0.01-0.06             Dixon2004                                          Drought mortality after that spell\n            \u001b[36mD50\u001b[39m     9.1                day        D       1.0-20.0           Aptekar2000                     Continuous submergence in seawater that halves survival\n        \u001b[36mk_flood\u001b[39m     5.3                  -        D        2.0-8.0           Aptekar2000                                   Steepness of survival against submergence\n            \u001b[36md_f\u001b[39m    0.35           m/season        D        0.3-0.6             Nolet2018           Burial per growing season that fully relieves soil-biota pressure\n          \u001b[36mtau_P\u001b[39m     0.9               year        D        0.5-2.0             Nolet2018                           Time scale of the build-up of soil-biota pressure\n         \u001b[36mP_seed\u001b[39m    0.02                  -        P      0.005-0.1       vanderStoel2002                              Pressure that seeds the build-up in fresh sand\n     \u001b[36mtau_relief\u001b[39m    0.25               year        P       0.08-1.0 deRooijvanderGoes1996                 Time scale of relief from soil-biota pressure by fresh sand\n          \u001b[36mp_max\u001b[39m     0.7                  -        P        0.4-0.9      vanderPutten1993                               Loss of vigour under full soil-biota pressure\n        \u001b[36mv_front\u001b[39m     0.4           m/season        M        0.3-0.5          Keijsers2015                                     Speed of the front of a spreading patch\n         \u001b[36mf_jump\u001b[39m     0.1                  -        P        0.0-0.3             Bonte2021                   Fraction of colonisations that jump beyond the neighbours\n  \u001b[36mjump_distance\u001b[39m     0.6                  m        P        0.4-1.0             Bonte2021                   Distance of a jump (three cells of 0.2 m in Bonte et al.)\n          \u001b[36mN_new\u001b[39m    0.05                  -        P       0.01-0.1                     -                  Shoot density of a newly colonised cell, relative to N_max\n          \u001b[36mh_new\u001b[39m     0.2                  m        P        0.1-0.4                     -                                     Canopy height of a newly colonised cell\n         \u001b[36mzr_new\u001b[39m     0.3                  m        P        0.1-0.5                     -                                      Rooted layer of a newly colonised cell\n  \u001b[36mestablishment\u001b[39m     0.0             1/year        P       0.0-0.05          Keijsers2016                           Establishment away from existing plants, per cell\n     \u001b[36mtau_r_grow\u001b[39m    0.02               year        D     0.005-0.05           Reijers2021             Response time of roots and rhizomes growing toward their target\n    \u001b[36mtau_r_decay\u001b[39m     2.0               year        P        1.0-4.0                     -           Response time of roots and rhizomes above their target (die-back)\n        \u001b[36mm_shoot\u001b[39m   0.001                 kg        D   0.0005-0.002           Reijers2021                                                         Dry mass of a shoot\n         \u001b[36mBr_max\u001b[39m     1.2             kg/m^2        P        0.5-2.0                     -                                               Largest root and rhizome mass\n            \u001b[36mrs0\u001b[39m     0.5                  -        M        0.3-0.7           Reijers2021                                             Root:shoot ratio without burial\n            \u001b[36mrs1\u001b[39m     1.0                  -        M        0.7-1.3           Reijers2021                                            Root:shoot ratio at burial rs_d1\n            \u001b[36mrs2\u001b[39m     2.5                  -        M        1.8-3.2           Reijers2021                                  Root:shoot ratio at burial rs_d2 and above\n          \u001b[36mrs_d1\u001b[39m    0.26           m/season        M       0.2-0.26           Reijers2021                                 Burial of the intermediate root:shoot ratio\n          \u001b[36mrs_d2\u001b[39m    0.52           m/season        M       0.3-0.52           Reijers2021                                      Burial of the largest root:shoot ratio\n         \u001b[36mzr_max\u001b[39m     1.5                  m        M        0.6-2.0         Homberger2024                                                        Deepest rooted layer\n         \u001b[36mf_fine\u001b[39m    0.17                  -        D        0.1-0.3        DeBattisti2020 Fraction of root mass in fine roots, fitted to the erosion of mature stands\n           \u001b[36mk_wu\u001b[39m     1.2                  -        M        1.0-1.2                Wu1979                                               Wu-Waldron orientation factor\n           \u001b[36mk_fb\u001b[39m     0.4                  -        D        0.2-0.6           Schwarz2013                             Fibre-bundle correction of the Wu-Waldron model\n            \u001b[36mT_r\u001b[39m    15.0                MPa        P      10.0-40.0                     -                                                   Tensile strength of roots\n       \u001b[36mrho_root\u001b[39m   350.0             kg/m^3        M    250.0-450.0     WalkerZinnert2022                                                         Root tissue density\n      \u001b[36mzr_min_cr\u001b[39m     0.3                  m        P        0.2-0.5                     -                   Least depth over which roots are spread for reinforcement\n          \u001b[36mc_max\u001b[39m     5.0                kPa        M        2.0-6.0            Figlus2014                                                  Largest root reinforcement\n           \u001b[36mtau0\u001b[39m     2.5                kPa        M        2.0-3.0           Erikson2007                                           Shear strength of moist bare sand\n            \u001b[36ma_f\u001b[39m   0.026                  m        M    0.013-0.026     LaporteFauret2024                                    Frontal width of a shoot per unit height\n          \u001b[36mkappa\u001b[39m     0.5                  -        P        0.3-1.0                     -                                 Share of frontal area not hidden by overlap\n         \u001b[36mm_beta\u001b[39m    50.0                  -        M      46.0-76.0            Walter2012                              Product of Raupach's m and beta for live grass","title":"Traits"},{"category":"section","location":"api_marram/#One-cell","page":"Marram grass","text":"","title":"One cell"},{"category":"section","location":"api_marram/#The-grid-model","page":"Marram grass","text":"These functions are not exported.","title":"The grid model"},{"category":"section","location":"api_marram/#Processes","page":"Marram grass","text":"These functions are not exported.","title":"Processes"},{"category":"type","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.MarramTraits","page":"Marram grass","text":"MarramTraits(; kwargs...)\n\nThe traits of marram grass (Calamagrostis arenaria): growing season, burial response, shoot growth and mortality, canopy height, root-zone water, flooding, soil-biota pressure, lateral spread, roots and reinforcement, and drag. Every keyword defaults to the value in marram_info, which also gives its unit, evidence grade (:M measured on marram, :D derived from measurements, :P a prior), plausible range and source. marram_traits reads traits from a TOML file.\n\nAn unknown keyword, or thresholds of soil moisture or season that are out of order, raise an ArgumentError.\n\nExamples\n\ntr = MarramTraits(; burial_opt=0.35)\ntr.N_max        # 470.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.MarramTraits"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.marram_traits","page":"Marram grass","text":"marram_traits(file)\nmarram_traits(p::AeolisParameters)\n\nMarram traits from a TOML file of name = value entries, optionally grouped in tables ([burial], [water], ...; the table names are ignored). Traits not in the file keep their defaults. For parameters, the file is p.veg_traits_file, and the defaults are used when it is nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.marram_traits"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.marram_info","page":"Marram grass","text":"marram_info([name])\n\nA table of every marram trait as (name, default, unit, evidence, range, source, description) named tuples, or the entry of one trait. Evidence is :M (measured on marram, or A. breviligulata where the source says so), :D (derived from measurements) or :P (a prior without a direct measurement); source is a key of the package bibliography.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.marram_info"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.write_marram_traits","page":"Marram grass","text":"write_marram_traits(file, tr::MarramTraits)\n\nWrite every trait of tr to a TOML file that marram_traits reads back, for example to give each member of an ensemble its own veg_traits_file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.write_marram_traits"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.marram_ranges","page":"Marram grass","text":"marram_ranges(; evidence=(:P,), add=())\n\nThe traits whose evidence grade is in evidence, and the traits named in add, with their plausible ranges, as (name, low, high) named tuples. Traits whose range is a single value are left out.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.marram_ranges"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.marram_sample","page":"Marram grass","text":"marram_sample(rng, n; evidence=(:P,), add=(), base=MarramTraits())\n\nn trait sets in which every trait of marram_ranges(; evidence, add) is drawn uniformly from its range and the others keep their values in base. Draws are independent, so they do not respect correlations that calibration would impose. Checking each draw against marram_experiment is a way to keep only plausible ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.marram_sample"},{"category":"type","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.MarramCell","page":"Marram grass","text":"MarramCell(; N=0, h=0, Br=0, zr=0.3, s=0.16, P=0, Dsub=0, tdry=0, zdot=0)\n\nThe state of marram grass in one cell:\n\nshoot density N (m⁻²);\ncanopy height above the bed h (m);\nroot and rhizome dry mass Br (kg m⁻²);\nrooted sand layer zr (m);\nroot-zone relative moisture s;\nsoil-biota pressure P (0-1);\ncurrent spells of submergence Dsub and of moisture below wilting tdry (days);\ntrailing mean rate of bed-level change zdot (m per day).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.MarramCell"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.marram_rates","page":"Marram grass","text":"marram_rates(cell, forcing, tr)\n\nEvery multiplier and rate of the marram model for cell (a MarramCell) under forcing. The forcing is a named tuple with the day of year doy, the rate of bed-level change dzb (m per day), and optionally the wind speed u (m s⁻¹).\n\nReturns a named tuple:\n\nphi: the season weight (season);\nd: the burial rate from the trailing mean cell.zdot (m per growing season);\nGb, fW, V: the burial, water and vigour multipliers;\ngrowth: the potential relative growth rate r phi fW Gb V (per day), with the shoot density approaching V N_max;\nm0, m_sm, m_dr, m_in: background, smothering, drought and flooding mortality (per day), and mortality, their sum;\nelong: shoot elongation (m per day);\nP_eq, Br_eq: the targets of pressure and roots;\ncr, lam: root reinforcement (kPa) and frontal area index.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.marram_rates"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.marram_step","page":"Marram grass","text":"marram_step(cell, forcing, tr, dt)\n\nThe MarramCell after dt days under forcing, a named tuple with:\n\ndoy, the day of year;\ndzb, the rate of bed-level change (m per day);\noptionally rain and pet (mm per day), which update the root-zone bucket;\noptionally s, a prescribed root-zone moisture, used instead of the bucket;\noptionally submerged, true while the bed is under the sea, or the fraction of the step it was under water. Submergence accumulates while it lasts and resets after a step without any; repeated tidal flooding therefore counts as exposure to seawater (evidence P);\noptionally u, the wind speed (m s⁻¹);\noptionally supply, a capillary supply from groundwater (mm per day).\n\nWithout rain and s, moisture keeps its value.\n\nThe order of the update:\n\nThe trailing mean of bed-level change.\nThe bucket, then the spells of drought and submergence.\nThe shoot density. Its exact logistic solution has the mortality of the step, where the flooding and drought losses are the exact integrals of their hazards over the step.\nCanopy height, roots (growing quickly toward their target and dying back slowly above it), the rooted layer and pressure (pressure_step; in a bare cell, pressure fades with tau_relief).\n\nEvery relaxation uses its exact solution, so states stay non-negative and bounded for any dt. A cell whose rooted layer is eroded away, or whose density falls below N_min, becomes bare.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.marram_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.simulate_marram","page":"Marram grass","text":"simulate_marram(cell, forcing, tr; days, start=Dates.Date(2020, 1, 1), dt=1.0)\n\nRun one cell for days days from the date start, with steps of dt days.\n\nforcing(t, date) returns the forcing of marram_step at day t (from 0) and date date, without doy; the day of year at the middle of the step is added.\ncell is a MarramCell and tr the MarramTraits.\n\nReturns a named tuple of vectors: the time t (days), the date, every state variable, and the derived quantities and multipliers of marram_rates (phi, d, Gb, fW, V, growth, mortality, cr and lam). The first entry is the initial state.\n\nExamples\n\nusing Dates\ntr = MarramTraits()\nsol = simulate_marram(MarramCell(; N=5.0, h=0.5), (t, date) -> (; dzb=1.4e-3), tr;\n                      days=365, start=Date(2016, 6, 24))\nsol.N[end]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.simulate_marram"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.marram_experiment","page":"Marram grass","text":"marram_experiment(name, tr=MarramTraits())\n\nRe-run a published experiment on marram grass with the single-cell model, and return the model's results next to the published ones. The experiments are:\n\n:reijers2021: the one-year mesocosm of Reijers et al. (2021). Four tussocks (32 shoots) in 6.25 m² of fresh sand were buried by 0, 2 or 4 cm every fortnight from late June 2016, that is 0, 0.26 or 0.52 m per growing season. Compares shoots per mesocosm, the root:shoot ratio and shoot elongation.\n:nolet2018: the growth response to burial over the 2015 growing season of a foredune planted in 2011 (Nolet et al. (2018)). Compares the model's response of a four-year-old stand, normalised to its peak, with the fitted quantile curves of NDVI and cover.\n:dixon2004: 28 days without water, then rewatering (Dixon et al. (2004)). Root-zone moisture is prescribed to be below wilting. Compares survival.\n:aptekar2000: survival after 1, 3, 7 and 13 days of continuous submergence in seawater, against the bud viability of rhizomes relative to the controls (Aptekar and Rejmánek (2000)).\n:lammers2024: four weeks of a hot July without rain, under open-sided tents, on transplants in an embryo dune (Lammers et al. (2024)). Compares shoot growth with the ambient treatment (12.8 mm of rain) for a sparse planting of 10 shoots m⁻² (an assumption); the paper found no difference.\n:homberger2024: the 132-day pot experiment of Homberger et al. (2024), with five marram shoots per pot of 0.081 m². Watering from 6.5 to 24.4 mm per week raised mean moisture by 2.2 % by volume per step. Plants died in pots with a mean of 3.3-4.8 %, and biomass rose linearly with moisture.\nThe model prescribes root-zone moisture at seven levels from 4 % (an assumption within the range where plants died) in steps of 2.2 %. Pot hydrology is not modelled: the pots held water above the field capacity of free-draining sand. Compares shoot growth with the observed death in the driest pots and the continued rise with moisture.\n:root_erosion: the reduction of wave-driven dune erosion by roots, c_r / (tau0 + c_r) (see dune_erosion!). It is computed in two ways:\nwith the root reinforcement of about 1 kPa that Figlus et al. (2014) measured in shear tests of their rooted flume dunes, where erosion fell by 33 % under regular and 8 % under irregular waves;\nwith the reinforcement of a model stand grown for five years under 0.3 m of burial per season, against the 36 % of intact cores of mature Ammophila under swash (De Battisti and Griffin (2020)) and up to 31 % in scaled flume experiments with marram (Kosmalla et al. (2026)).\n\nReturns a named tuple with name, description, and model and observed values whose fields depend on the experiment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.marram_experiment"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.initialize_marram!","page":"Marram grass","text":"initialize_marram!(s, m)\n\nSet up marram grass on the grid:\n\nthe traits from veg_traits_file (or the defaults), and the rainfall and potential evapotranspiration of veg_water_file;\nthe initial shoot density from veg_file, read as a fraction of N_max;\nthe canopy at h_new, roots at their target without burial, and a rooted layer of zr_new;\nroot-zone moisture at field capacity, no soil-biota pressure;\na colonisation threshold for every cell, from the model's random number generator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.initialize_marram!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.marram_cover!","page":"Marram grass","text":"marram_cover!(s, m)\n\nThe vegetation cover and height seen by the shear reduction: hveg is the canopy height and rhoveg the effective_cover of the frontal area index, which depends on the current wind speed through the reconfiguration of the shoots (frontal_area).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.marram_cover!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.marram_update!","page":"Marram grass","text":"marram_update!(s, m, t)\n\nAdvance marram grass after the time step that started at t. The time step, accelerated by accfac, is added to the time since the last update, and to the time under water of cells whose water level is above the bed. Once veg_interval has passed, every cell is advanced by marram_step, with:\n\nthe mean rate of bed-level change since the last update;\nthe day of year at the middle of the interval, from refdate and accelerated time;\nrainfall and potential evapotranspiration from veg_water_file, at the same time;\nthe fraction of the interval under water;\nthe current wind speed.\n\nWith groundwater, a root zone whose base is within 1 m of the water table is kept at field capacity, the capillary supply of Homberger et al. (2024). Below veg_min_elevation, plants are removed, as in AeoLiS. Bare cells are then colonised (marram_colonise!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.marram_update!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.marram_colonise!","page":"Marram grass","text":"marram_colonise!(s, m, phi, days)\n\nColonisation of bare cells over days days with season weight phi. A bare cell accumulates the hazard phi (v_front / L_eff) sum(w o_nb) / dx per day, plus establishment per year:\n\nL_eff = L_s - season_ramp is the number of full growing days in a year;\no_nb = min(N_nb / (N_new N_max), 1) is the occupancy of a neighbour. Rhizomes extend from any established clone, however sparse, so a front advances at about v_front whatever the grid spacing;\nthe sum runs over its neighbours along each axis (only along x on a transect), with weight 1 - f_jump, and over the cells jump_distance (in m) away, with weight f_jump.\n\nWhen the accumulated hazard reaches the cell's threshold (exponential, or 1 when veg_random is off), the cell is colonised with N_new N_max shoots, a canopy of h_new and a rooted layer of zr_new. The front speed v_front is that of patches on Ameland (Keijsers et al. (2015)), and the jumps those of Bonte et al. (2021).\n\nCells below veg_min_elevation, and cells that were under water for more than half the interval, are not colonised.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.marram_colonise!"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.season_length","page":"Marram grass","text":"season_length(tr)\n\nThe length of the growing season in days, season_end - season_start + 1 (183 for 1 April to 30 September). Burial rates are converted to m per growing season with it, the unit of Nolet et al. (2018).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.season_length"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.season","page":"Marram grass","text":"season(doy, tr)\n\nThe growing-season weight φ at day of year doy (fractional). It is 0 outside season_start-season_end, rises linearly over season_ramp days at the start, falls over season_ramp days at the end, and is 1 in between. Growth and lateral spread are multiplied by it; mortality and burial continue all year.\n\nThe window follows the NDVI time course of Nolet et al. (2018) and the April-August growth of Bonte et al. (2021) (evidence M); the ramps are a prior (P).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.season"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.seasonal_burial","page":"Marram grass","text":"seasonal_burial(zdot, tr)\n\nThe burial rate d in m per growing season of a rate of bed-level change zdot (m per day): zdot * season_length(tr). Negative for erosion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.seasonal_burial"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.burial_response","page":"Marram grass","text":"burial_response(d, tr)\n\nThe growth multiplier G_b of burial d (m per growing season): a split Gaussian exp(-(d - b)^2 / (2c^2)). The optimum is b = burial_opt; the width is c = burial_c_lo below the optimum and burial_c_hi above it.\n\nThe optimum is that of Nolet et al. (2018) (evidence M, one site and one season). The widths are fitted to the one-year mesocosm of Reijers et al. (2021) (evidence D). It had as many shoots without burial as at 0.26 m per season, but a third as many at 0.52 m, and a symmetric curve cannot do both.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.burial_response"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.elongation","page":"Marram grass","text":"elongation(d, tr)\n\nThe shoot elongation rate (m per day) at burial d (m per growing season): e0 + e1 * max(d, 0), a fit to the 0.3, 0.5 and 0.9 cm per day of Reijers et al. (2021) at 0, 0.26 and 0.52 m per season (evidence D).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.elongation"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.smother_hazard","page":"Marram grass","text":"smother_hazard(h, tr)\n\nThe mortality (per day) of shoots whose exposed canopy h is below h_min: max(1 - h / h_min, 0) / tau_sm, rising from 0 at h_min to 1 / tau_sm when the canopy is covered. Burial kills by covering the leaves: a single burial of about the canopy height is lethal, and 0.4 m leaves most plants alive (Maun and Lapierre (1984); Gilbert and Ripley (2010), A. breviligulata).\n\ntau_sm is fitted to the mesocosm of Reijers et al. (2021) (evidence D). There, 4 cm of sand on the plants every fortnight, through a winter without elongation, left a third as many shoots, which the authors attribute to buried leaves. h_min is a prior (P).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.smother_hazard"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.water_stress","page":"Marram grass","text":"water_stress(s, tr)\n\nThe water-stress multiplier f_W = 1 - ζ at root-zone relative moisture s, with ζ = ((s_star - s) / (s_star - s_w))^q_stress between wilting s_w and stomatal closure s_star. f_W = 1 above s_star and 0 below s_w. These are the form and values of Homberger et al. (2026) (evidence M).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.water_stress"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.evapotranspiration","page":"Marram grass","text":"evapotranspiration(s, pet, cover, tr)\n\nThe loss of root-zone water to evapotranspiration (mm per day) at relative moisture s.\n\nThis is the piecewise function of Laio et al. (2001), as used by Homberger et al. (2026): 0 below the hygroscopic point s_h, rising to E_w at wilting s_w, then linearly to the unstressed rate at s_star.\n\nThe unstressed rate is E_w plus the share cover of min(E_max, pet) - E_w, where cover (0-1) is the shoot density relative to N_max. Transpiration per unit ground area scales with leaf area, while evaporation from bare sand stays near E_w, because a dry surface layer insulates the root zone. This is why the transplants of Lammers et al. (2024) were unaffected when the top 10 cm dried out (evidence D).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.evapotranspiration"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.drainage","page":"Marram grass","text":"drainage(s, tr)\n\nDeep drainage from the root zone (mm per day) above field capacity: K_s (exp(b (s - s_fc)) - 1) / (exp(b (1 - s_fc)) - 1) (Laio et al. (2001)), with the values of Homberger et al. (2026). Their text and table disagree on b_leak (12.1 or 4.05).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.drainage"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.bucket_step","page":"Marram grass","text":"bucket_step(s, rain, pet, cover, dt, tr; supply=0.0)\n\nRoot-zone relative moisture after dt days of rainfall rain and potential evapotranspiration pet (mm per day), with canopy share cover. The balance is n Z_r ds/dt = I - E(s) - L(s), where I is rainfall less interception, plus any capillary supply (mm per day).\n\nRain is added first, and anything above saturation runs off. Losses are then integrated with steps that change s by at most 0.002, so s stays between s_h and 1 for any dt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.bucket_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.drought_hazard","page":"Marram grass","text":"drought_hazard(tdry, tr)\n\nDrought mortality (per day) after a spell of tdry days with root-zone moisture below wilting: k_dry once the spell is longer than t_dry, and 0 before. In Dixon et al. (2004), marram kept its leaf water content for about three weeks without water, and 80 % of plants recovered after four. This hazard gives 81 % (evidence D).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.drought_hazard"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.flood_survival","page":"Marram grass","text":"flood_survival(D, tr)\n\nThe share of plants surviving D days of continuous submergence in seawater, 1 / (1 + (D / D50)^k_flood). It is fitted to the bud viability of rhizomes of Aptekar and Rejmánek (2000), relative to their controls: 80 % after 7 days and 13 % after 13 (evidence D, rhizome fragments rather than whole plants).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.flood_survival"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.flood_hazard","page":"Marram grass","text":"flood_hazard(D, tr)\n\nThe instantaneous mortality (per day) after D days of continuous submergence, the hazard of flood_survival: k (D/D50)^k / (D (1 + (D/D50)^k)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.flood_hazard"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.pressure_step","page":"Marram grass","text":"pressure_step(P, d, dt, tr)\n\nSoil-biota pressure after dt days at burial d (m per growing season), using the exact solutions of its equations.\n\nBelow its target P_eq(d), pressure builds up logistically from the seed P_seed: dP/dt = (P + P_seed)(P_eq - P) / tau_P. Root-feeding nematodes colonise fresh sand within a month (van der Stoel et al. (2002)), but their populations take years to build up.\n\nAbove its target, fresh sand relieves pressure: dP/dt = (P_eq - P) / tau_relief. Regular burial keeps marram vigorous, and plants decline when sand stops arriving (de Rooij-van der Goes (1996)). tau_relief, a few months for roots to grow into new sand, is a prior (P).\n\nThe slow start of the build-up reconciles two observations:\n\none year on fresh sand without burial cost the mesocosm of Reijers et al. (2021) only 4 ± 2 % of its shoots;\nfour years after planting, the field response of Nolet et al. (2018) fell by half without burial.\n\ntau_P is fitted to both (evidence D); P_seed is a prior (P).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.pressure_step"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.pressure_target","page":"Marram grass","text":"pressure_target(d, tr)\n\nThe soil-biota pressure toward which a stand relaxes at burial d (m per growing season): clamp(1 - d / d_f, 0, 1). Fresh sand lets marram escape its root-feeding nematodes and pathogens, and pressure builds where sand stops arriving (van der Putten et al. (1993); de Rooij-van der Goes (1996); van der Stoel et al. (2002)). d_f is fitted to the fall of the field response of Nolet et al. (2018) below the optimum (evidence P).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.pressure_target"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.vigour","page":"Marram grass","text":"vigour(P, tr)\n\nThe vigour V = 1 - p_max P under soil-biota pressure P (evidence P). It multiplies both the growth rate and the shoot density that the roots can sustain, V N_max. A degenerating stand therefore thins, rather than only growing more slowly, as marram does on stable dunes (van der Putten et al. (1993)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.vigour"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.root_shoot_ratio","page":"Marram grass","text":"root_shoot_ratio(d, tr)\n\nThe root:shoot mass ratio toward which roots grow at burial d (m per growing season). It is linear through rs0, rs1 and rs2 at 0, rs_d1 and rs_d2, and constant outside. Buried marram puts more into roots: 0.5, 1.0 and 2.5 at 0, 0.26 and 0.52 m per season in Reijers et al. (2021) (evidence M).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.root_shoot_ratio"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.root_target","page":"Marram grass","text":"root_target(N, d, tr)\n\nThe root and rhizome mass (kg m⁻²) toward which roots relax: min(ρ_rs(d) m_shoot N, Br_max), with ρrs from [`rootshoot_ratio`](@ref).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.root_target"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.reinforcement","page":"Marram grass","text":"reinforcement(Br, zr, tr)\n\nRoot reinforcement of the rooted layer (kPa). This is the Wu-Waldron estimate k_wu T_r RAR, reduced by the fibre-bundle factor k_fb (Wu et al. (1979); Schwarz et al. (2013)), and capped at c_max.\n\nThe root area ratio RAR is the volume of fine roots per unit area, spread over the rooted layer: f_fine Br / (rho_root max(zr, zr_min_cr)), with the tissue density of Walker and Zinnert (2022). Measured reinforcement in dune sand is 0.5-5 kPa (Figlus et al. (2014)). The tensile strength T_r of marram roots has not been measured (evidence P).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.reinforcement"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.standing_fraction","page":"Marram grass","text":"standing_fraction(u, N)\n\nThe share of the blade height left standing in wind speed u (m s⁻¹) at shoot density N (m⁻²): clamp(94.45 - 4.12 u + 0.0195 N, 0, 100) / 100, Eq. 19 of Laporte-Fauret et al. (2024) (evidence M).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.standing_fraction"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.frontal_area","page":"Marram grass","text":"frontal_area(N, h, u, tr)\n\nThe frontal area index λ of the canopy: kappa N a_f h F(u, N). It combines the measured frontal area of marram shoots (Laporte-Fauret et al. (2024)), their reconfiguration in wind (standing_fraction), and a share kappa of area not hidden by overlapping leaves (evidence P).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.frontal_area"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.effective_cover","page":"Marram grass","text":"effective_cover(lam, tr, gamma_vegshear)\n\nThe vegetation cover rhoveg = min(m_beta lam / gamma_vegshear, 1). With it, AeoLiS's shear reduction 1 / sqrt(1 + gamma_vegshear rhoveg) equals the Raupach drag partition with the measured m beta of live grass (Walter et al. (2012)), without its (1 - m sigma lam) term and capped as by Durán and Moore (2013). The legacy model is the case where rhoveg is the cover.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.effective_cover"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.model_datetime","page":"Marram grass","text":"model_datetime(p::AeolisParameters, t)\n\nThe date and time of model time t (seconds since refdate). For vegetation, pass the accelerated time (t * accfac).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.model_datetime"},{"category":"function","location":"api_marram/#Aeolis.day_of_year","page":"Marram grass","text":"day_of_year(d::Dates.TimeType)\n\nThe day of the year of d as a real number: 1.0 at the start of 1 January, 1.5 at its noon.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.day_of_year"},{"category":"section","location":"api_parameters/#Parameters-and-state","page":"Parameters and state","text":"","title":"Parameters and state"},{"category":"type","location":"api_parameters/#Aeolis.AeolisParameters","page":"Parameters and state","text":"AeolisParameters(; kwargs...)\n\nThe parameters of an AeoLiS model: process switches, input data, grid and time settings, physical constants and numerical options. Every keyword has the name it has in an AeoLiS configuration file; parameter_info lists them with defaults, units and descriptions, and read_config builds parameters from a file.\n\nValues are converted to the parameter's type: numbers to Float64 or Int, strings to Symbol for method names, scalars to one-element vectors for list parameters. An unknown keyword raises an ArgumentError.\n\nExamples\n\np = AeolisParameters(; xgrid_file=collect(0.0:1.0:100.0), bed_file=zeros(101),\n                     wind_file=[0.0 10.0 270.0; 3600.0 10.0 270.0], tstop=3600.0)\np.grain_size   # [0.000225]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.AeolisParameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api_parameters/#Aeolis.parameter_info","page":"Parameters and state","text":"parameter_info([name])\n\nA table of every parameter as (name, type, default, unit, description) named tuples, or the entry of one parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.parameter_info"},{"category":"function","location":"api_parameters/#Aeolis.remake_parameters","page":"Parameters and state","text":"remake_parameters(p::AeolisParameters; kwargs...)\n\nA copy of p with the given parameters replaced.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.remake_parameters"},{"category":"type","location":"api_parameters/#Aeolis.AeolisState","page":"Parameters and state","text":"AeolisState{T}\n\nThe spatial state of an AeoLiS model: one array per variable, of size (nx+1, ny+1), (nx+1, ny+1, nfractions), (nx+1, ny+1, nlayers) or (nx+1, ny+1, nlayers, nfractions). Fields are read as s.zb or s[:zb]; state_info lists the variables with dimensions, units and descriptions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.AeolisState"},{"category":"function","location":"api_parameters/#Aeolis.state_info","page":"Parameters and state","text":"state_info([name])\n\nA table of every state variable as (name, dims, unit, description) named tuples, or the entry of one variable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Aeolis.state_info"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/11_callbacks_and_ensembles/#Callbacks-and-ensembles","page":"Callbacks and ensembles","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nA nourishment\nA storm that ends the run\nA sweep over grain sizes\nReferences","title":"Callbacks and ensembles"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/11_callbacks_and_ensembles/#Overview","page":"Callbacks and ensembles","text":"An AeolisProblem follows the SciML interface, so the tools for differential equations work with it: callbacks change the state or stop the run during a simulation, tstops make the model step to given times, remake changes parameters, and EnsembleProblem runs many variants, in parallel if threads are available. This vignette models a beach nourishment, a storm that ends a run, and a sweep over grain sizes.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing Plots\nusing Statistics\n\nx = collect(0.0:1.0:120.0)\nzb = @. -1.0 + 0.03 * x\nday = 86400.0\np = AeolisParameters(; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=zb, grain_size=0.0003,\n                     wind_file=[0.0 11.0 270.0; 30day 11.0 270.0],\n                     tide_file=[0.0 0.0; 30day 0.0], process_tide=true, dt=3600.0,\n                     tstop=4day, output_times=day / 4, output_vars=[:zb, :qs, :mass])\nprob = AeolisProblem(p)\n\nAeolisProblem with tspan (0.0, 345600.0)\nAeolisParameters\n  time: 0.0 to 345600.0 s, dt = 3600.0 s\n  scheme: euler_backward, solver: trunk\n  fractions: 1, layers: 3\n  enabled: process_wind, process_transport, process_bedupdate, process_threshold, th_grainsize, process_tide","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/11_callbacks_and_ensembles/#A-nourishment","page":"Callbacks and ensembles","text":"A DiscreteCallback takes a condition and an effect. Here, after two days, sand is placed on the upper beach, raising the bed level by up to 0.5 m around x = 65 m (the bed composition of the top layers is unchanged, so the new sand has the original grain size). tstops makes the model step to exactly two days.\n\nfunction nourish!(integ)\n    rise = @. 0.5 * exp(-((x - 65) / 8)^2)\n    integ[:zb] = integ[:zb] .+ rise\nend\nnourishment = DiscreteCallback((u, t, integ) -> t == 2day, nourish!)\nsol = solve(prob; callback=nourishment, tstops=[2day])\nzbt = transect(sol, :zb)\nplot(x, zbt[:, [1, 8, 9, end]]; lw=2, xlabel=\"x (m)\", ylabel=\"bed level (m)\",\n     label=permutedims([\"t = $(t / day) d\" for t in sol.t[[1, 8, 9, end]]]))\n\n(Image: )","title":"A nourishment"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/11_callbacks_and_ensembles/#A-storm-that-ends-the-run","page":"Callbacks and ensembles","text":"A callback can stop the simulation with terminate!. Here a wind series has a storm on the third day; the run ends when the flux at the landward end exceeds a limit.\n\nt = 0.0:3600.0:(10day)\nwind = hcat(t, [ti < 2.5day ? 9.0 : 16.0 for ti in t], fill(270.0, length(t)))\nstorm = remake(prob; wind_file=wind)\nlimit = DiscreteCallback((u, t, integ) -> integ[:qs][end - 1, 2, 1] > 0.02,\n                         integ -> terminate!(integ))\nssol = solve(storm; callback=limit)\ninteg = init(storm; callback=limit)\nsolve!(integ)\n(retcode=integ.retcode, stopped_at_hours=integ.t / 3600)\n\n(retcode = SciMLBase.ReturnCode.Terminated, stopped_at_hours = 61.0)","title":"A storm that ends the run"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/11_callbacks_and_ensembles/#A-sweep-over-grain-sizes","page":"Callbacks and ensembles","text":"EnsembleProblem runs trajectories made by prob_func, which receives the problem and a context with the trajectory number; EnsembleThreads runs them on the available threads.\n\nsizes = [0.00015, 0.0002, 0.0003, 0.0004, 0.0006]\neprob = EnsembleProblem(prob; prob_func=(pr, ctx) -> remake(pr; grain_size=sizes[ctx.sim_id]))\nesol = solve(eprob, EulerBackward(), Aeolis.SciMLBase.EnsembleThreads();\n             trajectories=length(sizes))\nsupply = [sum(transect(s, :qs)[end - 1, 1, 2:end]) * day / 4 for s in esol.u]\nplot(1000 .* sizes, supply; lw=2, marker=:circle, xlabel=\"grain size (mm)\",\n     ylabel=\"sand delivered landward in 4 days (kg/m)\", label=\"\")\n\n(Image: )\n\nFiner sand has a lower threshold and is transported more; the coarsest fraction barely moves at this wind speed.","title":"A sweep over grain sizes"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/11_callbacks_and_ensembles/#References","page":"Callbacks and ensembles","text":"","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_tides_waves_moisture/#Tides,-waves-and-moisture","page":"Tides, waves and moisture","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nThe beach\nWater levels and moisture\nThreshold and transport\nReferences","title":"Tides, waves and moisture"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_tides_waves_moisture/#Overview","page":"Tides, waves and moisture","text":"On a beach the supply of dry sand is controlled by the water. The tide and the runup of waves (Stockdon et al. 2006) wet the beach, wet sand needs a higher shear velocity to move, and the surface dries again with a time scale Tdry. This vignette sets up a beach and a dune under an onshore wind and follows the transport into the dune with and without moisture.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing Plots","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_tides_waves_moisture/#The-beach","page":"Tides, waves and moisture","text":"x = collect(0.0:1.0:200.0)\nzb = @. -2.0 + 0.04 * x + 3.0 * exp(-((x - 170) / 12)^2)\nt = collect(0.0:1800.0:(2 * 86400.0))\ntide = hcat(t, 1.8 .* sin.(2pi .* t ./ 44712.0))\nwaves = hcat(t, fill(1.0, length(t)), fill(8.0, length(t)))\nwind = hcat(t, fill(11.0, length(t)), fill(270.0, length(t)))\nplot(x, zb; lw=2, label=\"bed\", xlabel=\"x (m)\", ylabel=\"elevation (m)\")\nhline!([-1.8, 1.8]; ls=:dash, color=:gray, label=\"low and high spring tide\")\n\n(Image: )\n\nbase = (; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=zb, wind_file=wind, tide_file=tide, wave_file=waves,\n        process_tide=true, process_wave=true, process_runup=true, beach_slope=0.04,\n        grain_size=0.0003, dt=1800.0, tstop=86400.0, output_times=1800.0,\n        output_vars=[:zb, :zs, :TWL, :moist, :uth, :qs])\ndry = solve(AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; base...)))\nwet = solve(AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; base..., process_moist=true, th_moisture=true,\n                                           method_moist_process=:infiltration,\n                                           method_moist_threshold=:belly_johnson,\n                                           Tdry=3 * 3600.0)))\n\nAeolisSolution with 50 output times from 0.0 to 88200.0 s, retcode Success\n  saved: TWL, moist, qs, uth, zb, zs","title":"The beach"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_tides_waves_moisture/#Water-levels-and-moisture","page":"Tides, waves and moisture","text":"The total water level is the tide plus the runup of the waves; below it the beach is saturated, and above it the surface moisture decays:\n\nTWL = transect(wet, :TWL)[1, :]\nplot(wet.t ./ 3600, TWL; lw=2, label=\"total water level\", xlabel=\"time (h)\", ylabel=\"m\")\nplot!(wet.t ./ 3600, tide[1:length(wet.t), 2]; lw=2, ls=:dash, label=\"tide\")\n\n(Image: )\n\nmoist = transect(wet, :moist)\nheatmap(wet.t ./ 3600, x, moist; xlabel=\"time (h)\", ylabel=\"x (m)\",\n        colorbar_title=\"volumetric moisture\", ylims=(0, 150))\n\n(Image: )","title":"Water levels and moisture"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_tides_waves_moisture/#Threshold-and-transport","page":"Tides, waves and moisture","text":"With the Belly and Johnson formulation the threshold rises with the gravimetric moisture content and is infinite above 10 % volumetric moisture, so the wet part of the beach does not supply sand:\n\nk = argmax(TWL)\nplot(x, transect(dry, :uth)[:, 1, k]; lw=2, label=\"dry\", ylims=(0, 1),\n     xlabel=\"x (m)\", ylabel=\"threshold u*t (m/s) at high water\")\nplot!(x, min.(transect(wet, :uth)[:, 1, k], 1.0); lw=2, label=\"with moisture (capped at 1)\")\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe flux arriving at the dune foot, at x = 150 m, over the day:\n\ni = 151\nplot(dry.t ./ 3600, transect(dry, :qs)[i, 1, :]; lw=2, label=\"dry beach\",\n     xlabel=\"time (h)\", ylabel=\"flux at the dune foot (kg/m/s)\")\nplot!(wet.t ./ 3600, transect(wet, :qs)[i, 1, :]; lw=2, label=\"with moisture\")\n\n(Image: )\n\ntotal(sol) = sum(transect(sol, :qs)[i, 1, 2:end]) * 1800.0\n(dry=round(total(dry); digits=1), moist=round(total(wet); digits=1))\n\n(dry = 343.9, moist = 339.0)\n\nMoisture changes little here. With the AeoLiS default adaptation time T = 1 s and grains moving at the wind speed, the adaptation length is about 11 m, so even the narrow dry beach left at high water supplies the dune fully. The effect of moisture depends on the fetch: with a long adaptation time, which gives a long fetch, the wet beach matters.\n\nslow = (; base..., T=30.0)\ndry30 = solve(AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; slow...)))\nwet30 = solve(AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; slow..., process_moist=true, th_moisture=true,\n                                             method_moist_process=:infiltration,\n                                             method_moist_threshold=:belly_johnson,\n                                             Tdry=3 * 3600.0)))\n(dry=round(total(dry30); digits=1), moist=round(total(wet30); digits=1))\n\n(dry = 89.1, moist = 47.9)\n\nplot(dry30.t ./ 3600, transect(dry30, :qs)[i, 1, :]; lw=2, label=\"dry beach, T = 30 s\",\n     xlabel=\"time (h)\", ylabel=\"flux at the dune foot (kg/m/s)\")\nplot!(wet30.t ./ 3600, transect(wet30, :qs)[i, 1, :]; lw=2, label=\"with moisture, T = 30 s\")\nplot!(twinx(), wet30.t ./ 3600, transect(wet30, :TWL)[1, :]; lw=1, ls=:dash, color=:gray,\n      label=\"total water level\", ylabel=\"m\", legend=:bottomright)\n\n(Image: )","title":"Threshold and transport"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/05_tides_waves_moisture/#References","page":"Tides, waves and moisture","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Stockdon2006\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nStockdon, Hilary F., Rob A. Holman, Peter A. Howd, and Asbury H. Sallenger. 2006. “Empirical Parameterization of Setup, Swash, and Runup.” Coastal Engineering 53: 573–88. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.coastaleng.2005.12.005.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_sediment_sorting/#Sediment-sorting-and-armouring","page":"Sediment sorting and armouring","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nA mixed bed\nThe top layer coarsens\nSupply falls as the bed armours\nReferences","title":"Sediment sorting and armouring"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_sediment_sorting/#Overview","page":"Sediment sorting and armouring","text":"A natural beach holds a mixture of grain sizes. AeoLiS represents it with several sediment fractions in a stack of bed layers (Hoonhout and de Vries 2016): fine grains, with a lower threshold, are blown away first, the top layer coarsens, and once it is armoured with grains the wind cannot move, the supply of sand falls even though plenty remains below. This vignette follows that process on a flat transect.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing Plots","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_sediment_sorting/#A-mixed-bed","page":"Sediment sorting and armouring","text":"Three fractions of 0.2, 0.4 and 1.2 mm in equal proportions. At a wind speed of 9 m/s the shear velocity exceeds the thresholds of the two finer fractions but not that of the coarsest:\n\nd = [0.0002, 0.0004, 0.0012]\nx = collect(0.0:1.0:80.0)\np = AeolisParameters(; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=fill(2.0, length(x)), grain_size=d,\n                     grain_dist=[1 / 3, 1 / 3, 1 / 3], nlayers=5, layer_thickness=0.005,\n                     wind_file=[0.0 9.0 270.0; 86400.0 9.0 270.0], dt=600.0,\n                     tstop=24 * 3600.0, output_times=3600.0,\n                     output_vars=[:zb, :mass, :qs, :uth, :ustar])\nsol = solve(AeolisProblem(p))\nuth = transect(sol, :uth)[1, :, 2]\nustar = transect(sol, :ustar)[1, 2]\n(thresholds=round.(uth; digits=3), ustar=round(ustar; digits=3))\n\n(thresholds = [0.175, 0.248, 0.429], ustar = 0.401)","title":"A mixed bed"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_sediment_sorting/#The-top-layer-coarsens","page":"Sediment sorting and armouring","text":"The mass of each fraction in the top layer, upwind (x = 2 m), over time:\n\nmass = transect(sol, :mass)      # (x, layers, fractions, time)\nshare = mass[3, 1, :, :] ./ sum(mass[3, 1, :, :]; dims=1)\nplot(sol.t ./ 3600, permutedims(share); lw=2, xlabel=\"time (h)\",\n     ylabel=\"share of the top layer at x = 2 m\",\n     label=permutedims([\"$(1000d[k]) mm\" for k in 1:3]))\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe mean grain size of the top layer along the transect at the end:\n\nmtop = mass[:, 1, :, end]\ndmean = vec(sum(mtop .* permutedims(d); dims=2) ./ sum(mtop; dims=2))\nplot(x, 1000 .* dmean; lw=2, xlabel=\"x (m)\", ylabel=\"mean grain size of the top layer (mm)\",\n     label=\"\")\nhline!([1000 * sum(d) / 3]; ls=:dash, label=\"initial\")\n\n(Image: )","title":"The top layer coarsens"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_sediment_sorting/#Supply-falls-as-the-bed-armours","page":"Sediment sorting and armouring","text":"The flux of each fraction at the downwind end:\n\nqs = transect(sol, :qs)\nplot(sol.t ./ 3600, permutedims(qs[end - 1, :, :]); lw=2, xlabel=\"time (h)\",\n     ylabel=\"flux at x = 79 m (kg/m/s)\", label=permutedims([\"$(1000d[k]) mm\" for k in 1:3]))\n\n(Image: )\n\nA bed of fine sand only, with the same threshold-exceeding wind, keeps its supply:\n\nfine = solve(AeolisProblem(remake_parameters(p; grain_size=[0.0002], grain_dist=[1.0])))\nplot(sol.t ./ 3600, vec(sum(qs[end - 1, :, :]; dims=1)); lw=2, label=\"mixed bed\",\n     xlabel=\"time (h)\", ylabel=\"total flux at x = 79 m (kg/m/s)\")\nplot!(fine.t ./ 3600, transect(fine, :qs)[end - 1, 1, :]; lw=2, label=\"fine sand only\")\n\n(Image: )","title":"Supply falls as the bed armours"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/06_sediment_sorting/#References","page":"Sediment sorting and armouring","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Hoonhout2016\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nHoonhout, Bas M., and Sierd de Vries. 2016. “A Process-Based Model for Aeolian Sediment Transport and Spatiotemporal Varying Sediment Availability.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface 121: 1555–75. https://doi.org/10.1002/2015JF003692.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/07_vegetation/#Vegetation-and-foredunes","page":"Vegetation and foredunes","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nA vegetated backbeach\nShear reduction\nTrapping\nPlant response\nOkin’s shear reduction\nReferences","title":"Vegetation and foredunes"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/07_vegetation/#Overview","page":"Vegetation and foredunes","text":"Dune plants reduce the shear velocity at the bed, so sand blown off the beach is trapped among them. AeoLiS reduces the shear velocity by Raupach’s factor 1 / sqrt(1 + gamma rho) of the vegetation cover rho (Raupach 1992; Raupach et al. 1993), or by Okin’s reduction in the lee of each plant (Okin 2008). Plants grow towards a maximum height and respond to burial, either with the original AeoLiS rule or that of Durán and Moore (2013). This vignette grows a foredune behind a dry beach.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing Plots","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/07_vegetation/#A-vegetated-backbeach","page":"Vegetation and foredunes","text":"x = collect(0.0:1.0:150.0)\nzb = @. min(0.02 * x, 2.0)\nveg = @. ifelse(x > 100, 0.3, 0.0)\nday = 86400.0\nbase = (; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=zb, wind_file=[0.0 12.0 270.0; 365day 12.0 270.0],\n        grain_size=0.0003, dt=3600.0, tstop=10day, output_times=day,\n        output_vars=[:zb, :rhoveg, :hveg, :ustar, :qs], process_avalanche=true)\nbare = solve(AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; base...)))\nplants = solve(AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; base..., process_vegetation=true,\n                                              veg_file=veg, gamma_vegshear=16.0,\n                                              hveg_max=1.0, V_ver=2.0, dzb_opt=0.5,\n                                              avg_time=day)))\n\nAeolisSolution with 11 output times from 0.0 to 864000.0 s, retcode Success\n  saved: hveg, qs, rhoveg, ustar, zb","title":"A vegetated backbeach"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/07_vegetation/#Shear-reduction","page":"Vegetation and foredunes","text":"plot(x, transect(bare, :ustar)[:, 2]; lw=2, label=\"bare\", xlabel=\"x (m)\",\n     ylabel=\"shear velocity u* (m/s)\")\nplot!(x, transect(plants, :ustar)[:, 2]; lw=2, label=\"vegetated beyond x = 100 m\")\n\n(Image: )\n\nWith a cover of 0.3 and gamma = 16, the shear velocity falls by a factor 1 / sqrt(1 + 16 * 0.3):\n\n1 / sqrt(1 + 16 * 0.3)\n\n0.41522739926869984","title":"Shear reduction"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/07_vegetation/#Trapping","page":"Vegetation and foredunes","text":"Sand arriving at the vegetation is deposited where the shear velocity drops below the threshold, building a foredune at its seaward edge:\n\nplot(x, transect(bare, :zb)[:, end]; lw=2, label=\"bare, day 10\", xlabel=\"x (m)\",\n     ylabel=\"bed level (m)\")\nplot!(x, transect(plants, :zb)[:, end]; lw=2, label=\"vegetated, day 10\")\nplot!(x, zb; lw=1, ls=:dash, color=:gray, label=\"initial\")\n\n(Image: )\n\ndz = transect(plants, :zb)[:, end] .- zb\n(max_accretion=round(maximum(dz); digits=3), at_x=x[argmax(dz)])\n\n(max_accretion = 0.255, at_x = 101.0)","title":"Trapping"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/07_vegetation/#Plant-response","page":"Vegetation and foredunes","text":"The vegetation height changes with the growth potential V_ver, less the effect of burial away from the optimum dzb_opt; the cover follows the height:\n\nplot(x, transect(plants, :hveg)[:, [1, 3, 6, 11]]; lw=2, xlabel=\"x (m)\",\n     ylabel=\"vegetation height (m)\", label=[\"day 0\" \"day 2\" \"day 5\" \"day 10\"],\n     xlims=(90, 150))\n\n(Image: )","title":"Plant response"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/07_vegetation/#Okin’s-shear-reduction","page":"Vegetation and foredunes","text":"The Okin model reduces the shear velocity downwind of each plant, with a reduction that recovers over some twenty plant heights. Under continuous cover the reductions of the plants upwind add up, so the shear velocity falls to the model’s floor of 0.01 m/s within a few metres of the edge of the vegetation, where Raupach’s factor gives a uniform reduction:\n\nokin = solve(AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; base..., process_vegetation=true,\n                                            veg_file=veg, vegshear_type=:okin, tstop=day)))\nplot(x, transect(plants, :ustar)[:, 2]; lw=2, label=\"Raupach\", xlabel=\"x (m)\",\n     ylabel=\"shear velocity u* (m/s)\", xlims=(90, 150))\nplot!(x, transect(okin, :ustar)[:, 2]; lw=2, label=\"Okin\")\n\n(Image: )","title":"Okin’s shear reduction"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/07_vegetation/#References","page":"Vegetation and foredunes","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Duran2013\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nDurán, Orencio, and Laura J. Moore. 2013. “Vegetation Controls on the Maximum Size of Coastal Dunes.” Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 110: 17217–22. https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1307580110.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Okin2008\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nOkin, Gregory S. 2008. “A New Model of Wind Erosion in the Presence of Vegetation.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface 113: F02S10. https://doi.org/10.1029/2007JF000758.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Raupach1992\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nRaupach, M. R. 1992. “Drag and Drag Partition on Rough Surfaces.” Boundary-Layer Meteorology 60: 375–95. https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00155203.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Raupach1993\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nRaupach, M. R., D. A. Gillette, and J. F. Leys. 1993. “The Effect of Roughness Elements on Wind Erosion Threshold.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Atmospheres 98: 3023–29. https://doi.org/10.1029/92JD01922.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/08_sand_fences/#Sand-fences","page":"Sand fences","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nSheltering\nDeposition and burial\nReferences","title":"Sand fences"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/08_sand_fences/#Overview","page":"Sand fences","text":"Sand fences are a common way to build dunes. In AeoLiS a fence reduces the shear velocity in its lee following Okin’s model of porous roughness elements (Okin 2008), computed on a grid aligned with the wind, and is buried as sand accumulates around it; a fence lower than 0.1 m no longer acts. This vignette places a fence on a beach and compares the deposition with an open beach.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing Plots\n\nx = collect(0.0:1.0:120.0)\nzb = fill(2.0, length(x))\nfence = zeros(length(x))\nfence[61] = 1.0                   # a 1 m fence at x = 60 m\nbase = (; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=zb, wind_file=[0.0 12.0 270.0; 30 * 86400.0 12.0 270.0],\n        grain_size=0.0003, dt=1800.0, tstop=2 * 86400.0, output_times=6 * 3600.0,\n        output_vars=[:zb, :ustar, :fence_height, :qs], dx=1.0)\nopen_beach = solve(AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; base...)))\nfenced = solve(AeolisProblem(AeolisParameters(; base..., process_fences=true,\n                                              fence_file=fence)))\n\nAeolisSolution with 9 output times from 0.0 to 172800.0 s, retcode Success\n  saved: fence_height, qs, ustar, zb","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/08_sand_fences/#Sheltering","page":"Sand fences","text":"plot(x, transect(open_beach, :ustar)[:, 2]; lw=2, label=\"open\", xlabel=\"x (m)\",\n     ylabel=\"shear velocity u* (m/s)\")\nplot!(x, transect(fenced, :ustar)[:, 2]; lw=2, label=\"with a fence at x = 60 m\")\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe shear velocity drops behind the fence and recovers over some ten fence heights.","title":"Sheltering"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/08_sand_fences/#Deposition-and-burial","page":"Sand fences","text":"dz = transect(fenced, :zb) .- zb\nplot(x, dz[:, 2:end]; xlabel=\"x (m)\", ylabel=\"bed level change (m)\",\n     label=permutedims([\"t = $(Int(t / 3600)) h\" for t in fenced.t[2:end]]), xlims=(30, 110))\n\n(Image: )\n\nplot(fenced.t ./ 3600, transect(fenced, :fence_height)[61, :]; lw=2, marker=:circle,\n     xlabel=\"time (h)\", ylabel=\"exposed fence height (m)\", label=\"\")\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe deposit holds sand that the wind brought to the fence:\n\nrhob = 2650.0 * (1 - 0.4)\ntrapped = sum(max.(dz[45:80, end], 0.0)) * rhob       # kg per metre of fence, 1 m cells\nround(trapped; digits=1)\n\n290.1\n\nOn this beach the bed downwind of the fence is as loose as the bed upwind, so the wind picks up a new load past the fence and the flux leaving the transect barely changes:\n\nout(sol) = sum(transect(sol, :qs)[end - 1, 1, 2:end]) * 6 * 3600.0\n(open=round(out(open_beach); digits=1), fenced=round(out(fenced); digits=1))\n\n(open = 1058.1, fenced = 1053.6)\n\nA fence protects what lies behind it only where the supply there is limited, for example a vegetated or armoured backbeach.","title":"Deposition and burial"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/08_sand_fences/#References","page":"Sand fences","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Okin2008\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nOkin, Gregory S. 2008. “A New Model of Wind Erosion in the Presence of Vegetation.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface 113: F02S10. https://doi.org/10.1029/2007JF000758.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"#Aeolis.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Julia port of AeoLiS (de Vries et al., 2023), the process-based model of aeolian sediment transport with spatially and temporally varying sediment availability (Hoonhout and de Vries, 2016).\n\nA simulation is an AeolisProblem of AeolisParameters, solved with the SciML interface:\n\nusing Aeolis\np = read_config(\"aeolis.txt\")          # or AeolisParameters(; xgrid_file=..., ...)\nsol = solve(AeolisProblem(p), EulerBackward(Trunk()))\nsol[:zb]                                # bed level at every output time\n\nThe model reproduces aeolis-python 3.1.0rc1 to round-off; behaviours of AeoLiS that look unintended are kept so that results agree, and documented where they occur.","title":"Aeolis.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Tutorials","page":"Home","text":"The tutorials are the package's vignettes and run from simple to advanced. HTML and PDF versions, and the Quarto sources, are in vignettes/ in the repository.\n\nVignette Topic\n01 Getting started parameters, problems, solutions and plots on a dry transect; fetch and erosion\n02 AeoLiS configuration files reading and writing aeolis.txt, run_config, netCDF output, agreement with aeolis-python\n03 Transport formulations thresholds, six saturated-flux formulations, grain velocities\n04 Fetch and supply limitation the fetch ODE with OrdinaryDiffEq, numerical diffusion, a non-erodible layer\n05 Tides, waves and moisture water levels, runup, infiltration and the moisture threshold\n06 Sediment sorting and armouring several fractions in bed layers, coarsening and the fall of supply\n07 Vegetation and foredunes Raupach and Okin shear reduction, trapping, growth and burial\n08 Sand fences sheltering, deposition and burial of a fence\n09 Dunes in two dimensions spectral shear, the separation bubble, a migrating hill\n10 Numerical schemes and solvers implicit, Crank-Nicolson and explicit schemes; trunk, pieter and steady-state solvers; LinearSolve algorithms\n11 Callbacks and ensembles a nourishment and a storm with SciMLBase callbacks, tstops, an ensemble sweep\n12 Calibration fitting transport parameters to flux observations with Optimization.jl\n13 Marram grass a model of marram grass in one cell, and the published experiments it is calibrated to\n14 A foredune built by marram grass marram grass on a transect: trapping, burial, spread, and planting density\n15 Uncertainty in the marram grass model Sobol indices of the priors, history matching against the calibration experiments, and an ensemble of foredunes and storms","title":"Tutorials"},{"category":"section","location":"#Processes-and-formulations","page":"Home","text":"Process Formulation\nShear perturbation by topography (Weng et al., 1991) (spectral), (Kroy et al., 2002) (quasi2d)\nGrain velocity (Durán et al., 2011)\nEquilibrium transport (Bagnold, 1937) and others, (van Rijn and Strypsteen, 2020)\nRunup (Stockdon et al., 2006)\nDune erosion (Palmsten and Holman, 2012)\nShear reduction by vegetation and roughness (Raupach, 1992), (Raupach et al., 1993), (Okin, 2008)\nVegetation growth AeoLiS, or (Durán and Moore, 2013)\nMarram grass (Marram grass) (Nolet et al., 2018), (Reijers et al., 2021), (Homberger et al., 2026), (van der Putten et al., 1993) and others","title":"Processes and formulations"},{"category":"section","location":"#Array-layout","page":"Home","text":"Spatial arrays are (nx+1, ny+1), with layers and fractions after them: x is the first dimension. A transect (a vector xgrid_file) becomes a grid of three identical rows, as in AeoLiS; transect returns the middle row of saved output.","title":"Array layout"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/12_calibration/#Calibration","page":"Calibration","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nObservations\nObjective\nFitting\nReferences","title":"Calibration"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/12_calibration/#Overview","page":"Calibration","text":"Transport constants such as Bagnold’s Cb and the adaptation time T are rarely known for a site and are calibrated against measurements. Because an AeolisProblem can be remade with new parameters, any optimiser can drive it; this vignette fits Cb and T to synthetic observations of sand flux with Optimization.jl and a derivative-free Nelder-Mead search from Optim.jl; the model’s iterations for supply limitation make its output piecewise smooth in the parameters, so a method without gradients is a robust choice.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing Optimization\nusing OptimizationOptimJL\nusing Plots\nusing Random","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/12_calibration/#Observations","page":"Calibration","text":"A flat sandy transect with two flux sensors: one at x = 5 m, inside the zone where the flux is still adapting to the wind, and one at x = 40 m, where it is saturated. The “true” parameters are Cb = 1.8 and T = 0.6 s; the observations are hourly fluxes with 5 % noise.\n\nx = collect(0.0:1.0:60.0)\nt = 0.0:1800.0:86400.0\nwind = hcat(t, 9 .+ 4 .* sin.(t ./ 7000.0) .^ 2, fill(270.0, length(t)))\nbase = AeolisParameters(; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=fill(2.0, length(x)), grain_size=0.0003,\n                        wind_file=wind, dt=900.0, tstop=12 * 3600.0, output_times=3600.0,\n                        output_vars=[:qs])\nprob = AeolisProblem(base)\nsensors = [6, 41]\nmodel_flux(Cb, T) = transect(solve(remake(prob; Cb, T)), :qs)[sensors, 1, 2:end]\ntruth = model_flux(1.8, 0.6)\nrng = Xoshiro(1)\nobs = truth .* (1 .+ 0.05 .* randn(rng, size(truth)))\nplot(1:size(obs, 2), permutedims(obs); marker=:circle, lw=0, xlabel=\"hour\",\n     ylabel=\"observed flux (kg/m/s)\", label=[\"x = 5 m\" \"x = 40 m\"])\n\n(Image: )","title":"Observations"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/12_calibration/#Objective","page":"Calibration","text":"The sum of squared relative errors; parameters are optimised on a log scale so they stay positive:\n\nfunction loss(θ, _)\n    Cb, T = exp.(θ)\n    pred = model_flux(Cb, T)\n    return sum(abs2, (pred .- obs) ./ (obs .+ 1e-6))\nend\n[(T=T, loss=round(loss(log.([1.8, T]), nothing); sigdigits=3)) for T in (0.1, 0.3, 0.6, 1.0, 2.0)]\n\n5-element Vector{@NamedTuple{T::Float64, loss::Float64}}:\n (T = 0.1, loss = 11.1)\n (T = 0.3, loss = 2.97)\n (T = 0.6, loss = 0.102)\n (T = 1.0, loss = 1.04)\n (T = 2.0, loss = 4.55)","title":"Objective"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/12_calibration/#Fitting","page":"Calibration","text":"f = OptimizationFunction(loss)\nop = OptimizationProblem(f, log.([1.0, 2.0]))\nres = solve(op, NelderMead(); maxiters=150)\nfitted = exp.(res.u)\n(Cb=round(fitted[1]; digits=3), T=round(fitted[2]; digits=3), loss=res.objective)\n\n(Cb = 1.748, T = 0.598, loss = 0.08233236790948349)\n\npred = model_flux(fitted...)\nplot(1:size(obs, 2), permutedims(obs); marker=:circle, lw=0, label=[\"obs 5 m\" \"obs 40 m\"],\n     xlabel=\"hour\", ylabel=\"flux (kg/m/s)\")\nplot!(1:size(obs, 2), permutedims(pred); lw=2, label=[\"fit 5 m\" \"fit 40 m\"])\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe sensor inside the adaptation zone carries the information about T, and the sensor where the flux is saturated the information about Cb. With sensors only far downwind, the flux is saturated everywhere they measure and T cannot be identified.","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/12_calibration/#References","page":"Calibration","text":"","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/09_dunes_in_two_dimensions/#Dunes-in-two-dimensions","page":"Dunes in two dimensions","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nA sand hill\nShear over the hill\nMigration\nReferences","title":"Dunes in two dimensions"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/09_dunes_in_two_dimensions/#Overview","page":"Dunes in two dimensions","text":"Over a dune the wind accelerates up the stoss slope and slows down in the lee. AeoLiS computes this perturbation of the shear stress with the spectral method of Weng et al. (1991) on a computational grid aligned with the wind, after smoothing steep lee slopes with a separation bubble, under which the shear stress vanishes (Kroy et al. 2002). This vignette runs a small dune on a non-erodible floor, a setting that makes it migrate as a barchan.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing Plots","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/09_dunes_in_two_dimensions/#A-sand-hill","page":"Dunes in two dimensions","text":"xs = 0.0:1.0:100.0\nys = 0.0:1.0:60.0\nX = [x for x in xs, y in ys]\nY = [y for x in xs, y in ys]\nZ = @. 3.0 * exp(-((X - 30)^2 + (Y - 30)^2) / 120)\nheatmap(xs, ys, permutedims(Z); aspect_ratio=:equal, xlabel=\"x (m)\", ylabel=\"y (m)\",\n        colorbar_title=\"bed level (m)\")\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe hill sits on a non-erodible floor at 0.01 m, and the wind blows along x at 12 m/s.\n\nday = 86400.0\np = AeolisParameters(; xgrid_file=X, ygrid_file=Y, bed_file=Z, ne_file=fill(0.01, size(Z)),\n                     th_nelayer=true, wind_file=[0.0 12.0 270.0; 365day 12.0 270.0],\n                     process_shear=true, process_separation=true, process_avalanche=true,\n                     solver=:pieter, boundary_offshore=:constant, boundary_onshore=:constant,\n                     boundary_lateral=:circular, nlayers=1, layer_thickness=0.05,\n                     grain_size=0.00025, method_grainspeed=:duran, L=50.0, l=1.0,\n                     buffer_width=10.0, dt=3600.0, tstop=3day, output_times=day / 2,\n                     output_vars=[:zb, :ustar, :zsep, :hsep, :qs])\nsol = solve(AeolisProblem(p))\n\nAeolisSolution with 7 output times from 0.0 to 259200.0 s, retcode Success\n  saved: hsep, qs, ustar, zb, zsep","title":"A sand hill"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/09_dunes_in_two_dimensions/#Shear-over-the-hill","page":"Dunes in two dimensions","text":"The shear velocity rises over the crest and is zero under the separation bubble in the lee:\n\nheatmap(xs, ys, permutedims(sol[:ustar][:, :, 2]); aspect_ratio=:equal, xlabel=\"x (m)\",\n        ylabel=\"y (m)\", colorbar_title=\"u* (m/s)\")\ncontour!(xs, ys, permutedims(sol[:zb][:, :, 2]); levels=0.5:0.5:3, color=:white)\n\n(Image: )\n\nj = 31\nplot(xs, sol[:zb][:, j, 2]; lw=2, label=\"bed\", xlabel=\"x (m)\", ylabel=\"elevation (m)\")\nplot!(xs, sol[:zsep][:, j, 2]; lw=2, ls=:dash, label=\"separation bubble\")\nplot!(twinx(), xs, sol[:ustar][:, j, 2]; lw=2, color=:black, label=\"u*\", ylabel=\"u* (m/s)\",\n      legend=:topright)\n\n(Image: )","title":"Shear over the hill"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/09_dunes_in_two_dimensions/#Migration","page":"Dunes in two dimensions","text":"The hill loses sand on its stoss side and gains it in the lee, so its crest moves downwind:\n\nplot(xs, sol[:zb][:, j, 1:2:end]; lw=2, xlabel=\"x (m)\", ylabel=\"elevation along y = 30 m\",\n     label=permutedims([\"t = $(t / day) d\" for t in sol.t[1:2:end]]))\n\n(Image: )\n\ncrest = [xs[argmax(sol[:zb][:, j, k])] for k in eachindex(sol.t)]\n(times_days=sol.t ./ day, crest_x=crest)\n\n(times_days = [0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5, 3.0], crest_x = [30.0, 31.0, 32.0, 33.0, 33.0, 34.0, 35.0])","title":"Migration"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/09_dunes_in_two_dimensions/#References","page":"Dunes in two dimensions","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Kroy2002\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nKroy, Klaus, Gerd Sauermann, and Hans J. Herrmann. 2002. “Minimal Model for Aeolian Sand Dunes.” Physical Review E 66: 031302. https://doi.org/10.1103/PhysRevE.66.031302.\n\n</div>\n\n<div id=\"ref-Weng1991\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nWeng, W. S., J. C. R. Hunt, D. J. Carruthers, et al. 1991. “Air Flow and Sand Transport over Sand-Dunes.” In Aeolian Grain Transport. Acta Mechanica Supplementum. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-7091-6703-8_1.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"Aptekar, R. and Rejmánek, M. (2000). The effect of sea-water submergence on rhizome bud viability of the introduced Ammophila arenaria and the native Leymus mollis in California. Journal of Coastal Conservation 6, 107–111.\n\n\n\nBagnold, R. A. (1937). The Transport of Sand by Wind. The Geographical Journal 89, 409–438.\n\n\n\nBonte, D.; Batsleer, F.; Provoost, S.; Reijers, V.; Vandegehuchte, M. L.; Van De Walle, R.; Dan, S.; Matheve, H.; Rauwoens, P.; Strypsteen, G.; Suzuki, T.; Verwaest, T. and Hillaert, J. (2021). Biomorphogenic feedbacks and the spatial organization of a dominant grass steer dune development. Frontiers in Ecology and Evolution 9, 761336.\n\n\n\nDixon, J. M.; Hilton, M. J. and Bannister, P. (2004). Desmoschoenus spiralis displacement by Ammophila arenaria: the role of drought. New Zealand Journal of Ecology 28, 207–213.\n\n\n\nDurán, O.; Claudin, P. and Andreotti, B. (2011). On aeolian transport: Grain-scale interactions, dynamical mechanisms and scaling laws. Aeolian Research 3, 243–270.\n\n\n\nDurán, O. and Moore, L. J. (2013). Vegetation controls on the maximum size of coastal dunes. Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences 110, 17217–17222.\n\n\n\nErikson, L. H.; Larson, M. and Hanson, H. (2007). Laboratory investigation of beach scarp and dune recession due to notching and subsequent failure. Marine Geology 245, 1–19.\n\n\n\nFeagin, R. A.; Innocenti, R. A.; Bond, H.; Wengrove, M.; Huff, T. P.; Lomonaco, P.; Tsai, B.; Puleo, J.; Pontiki, M.; Figlus, J.; Chavez, V. and Silva, R. (2023). Does vegetation accelerate coastal dune erosion during extreme events? Science Advances 9, eadg7135.\n\n\n\nFiglus, J.; Sigren, J. M.; Armitage, A. R. and Tyler, R. C. (2014). Erosion of vegetated coastal dunes. Coastal Engineering Proceedings 1, sediment.20.\n\n\n\nGilbert, M. E. and Ripley, B. S. (2010). Resolving the differences in plant burial responses. Austral Ecology 35, 53–59.\n\n\n\nde Rooij-van der Goes, P. C. (1996). Soil-borne plant pathogens of Ammophila arenaria in coastal foredunes. Ph.D. Thesis, Wageningen Agricultural University.\n\n\n\nHomberger, J.-M.; Lynch, A.; Riksen, M. and Limpens, J. (2024). Growth response of dune-building grasses to precipitation. Ecohydrology 18, e2634.\n\n\n\nHomberger, J.-M.; Strypsteen, G.; Marcotte, A. L.; van Rosmalen, S.; Riksen, M. and Limpens, J. (2026). Plant-water interactions shape coastal dune evolution in a changing climate. Journal of Hydrology 668, 134921.\n\n\n\nHoonhout, B. M. and de Vries, S. (2016). A process-based model for aeolian sediment transport and spatiotemporal varying sediment availability. Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface 121, 1555–1575.\n\n\n\nKeijsers, J. G.; De Groot, A. V. and Riksen, M. J. (2015). Vegetation and sedimentation on coastal foredunes. Geomorphology 228, 723–734.\n\n\n\nKosmalla, V.; Lojek, O.; Ahrenbeck, L.; Mehrtens, B.; Schweiger, C.; Schürenkamp, D. and Goseberg, N. (2026). Vegetation effects on dune erosion under wave collision: influence of planting density, biomass distribution and arrangement in scaled experiments. Coastal Engineering 204, 104899.\n\n\n\nKroy, K.; Sauermann, G. and Herrmann, H. J. (2002). Minimal model for aeolian sand dunes. Physical Review E 66, 031302.\n\n\n\nLaio, F.; Porporato, A.; Ridolfi, L. and Rodriguez-Iturbe, I. (2001). Plants in water-controlled ecosystems: active role in hydrologic processes and response to water stress: II. Probabilistic soil moisture dynamics. Advances in Water Resources 24, 707–723.\n\n\n\nLammers, C.; Berghuis, P. M.; Mayor, A. G.; Reijers, V. C.; Rietkerk, M. and van der Heide, T. (2024). Extreme heat and drought did not affect interspecific interactions between dune grasses. Estuarine, Coastal and Shelf Science 311, 109020.\n\n\n\nLaporte-Fauret, Q.; Wengrove, M.; Ruggiero, P.; Hacker, S. D.; Cohn, N.; Zarnetske, P. L. and Piercy, C. D. (2024). A new approach to account for species-specific sand capture by plants in an aeolian sediment transport and coastal dune building model. Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface 129, e2024JF007867.\n\n\n\nMaun, M. A. and Lapierre, J. (1984). The effects of burial by sand on Ammophila breviligulata. Journal of Ecology 72, 827–839.\n\n\n\nNolet, C.; van Puijenbroek, M.; Suomalainen, J.; Limpens, J. and Riksen, M. (2018). UAV-imaging to model growth response of marram grass to sand burial: implications for coastal dune development. Aeolian Research 31, 50–61.\n\n\n\nOkin, G. S. (2008). A new model of wind erosion in the presence of vegetation. Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface 113, F02S10.\n\n\n\nPalmsten, M. L. and Holman, R. A. (2012). Laboratory investigation of dune erosion using stereo video. Coastal Engineering 60, 123–135.\n\n\n\nvan der Putten, W. H.; van Dijk, C. and Peters, B. A. (1993). Plant-specific soil-borne diseases contribute to succession in foredune vegetation. Nature 362, 53–56.\n\n\n\nRaupach, M. R. (1992). Drag and drag partition on rough surfaces. Boundary-Layer Meteorology 60, 375–395.\n\n\n\nRaupach, M. R.; Gillette, D. A. and Leys, J. F. (1993). The effect of roughness elements on wind erosion threshold. Journal of Geophysical Research: Atmospheres 98, 3023–3029.\n\n\n\nReijers, V. C.; Hoeks, S.; van Belzen, J.; Siteur, K.; de Rond, A. J.; van de Ven, C. N.; Lammers, C.; van de Koppel, J. and van der Heide, T. (2021). Sediment availability provokes a shift from Brownian to Lévy-like clonal expansion in a dune building grass. Ecology Letters 24, 258–268.\n\n\n\nSchwarz, M.; Giadrossich, F. and Cohen, D. (2013). Modeling root reinforcement using a root-failure Weibull survival function. Hydrology and Earth System Sciences 17, 4367–4377.\n\n\n\nStockdon, H. F.; Holman, R. A.; Howd, P. A. and Sallenger, A. H. (2006). Empirical parameterization of setup, swash, and runup. Coastal Engineering 53, 573–588.\n\n\n\nvan der Stoel, C. D.; van der Putten, W. H. and Duyts, H. (2002). Development of a negative plant-soil feedback in the expansion zone of the clonal grass Ammophila arenaria following root formation and nematode colonization. Journal of Ecology 90, 978–988.\n\n\n\nWalker, S. L. and Zinnert, J. C. (2022). Whole plant traits of coastal dune vegetation and implications for interactions with dune dynamics. Ecosphere 13, e4065.\n\n\n\nWalter, B.; Gromke, C. and Lehning, M. (2012). Shear-stress partitioning in live plant canopies and modifications to Raupach's model. Boundary-Layer Meteorology 144, 217–241.\n\n\n\nWeng, W. S.; Hunt, J. C.; Carruthers, D. J.; Warren, A.; Wiggs, G. F.; Livingstone, I. and Castro, I. (1991). Air flow and sand transport over sand-dunes. In: Aeolian Grain Transport, Acta Mechanica Supplementum; pp. 1–22.\n\n\n\nWu, T. H.; McKinnell III, W. P. and Swanston, D. N. (1979). Strength of tree roots and landslides on Prince of Wales Island, Alaska. Canadian Geotechnical Journal 16, 19–33.\n\n\n\nDe Battisti, D. and Griffin, J. N. (2020). Below-ground biomass of plants, with a key contribution of buried shoots, increases foredune resistance to wave swash. Annals of Botany 125, 325–334.\n\n\n\nde Vries, S.; Hallin, C.; van IJzendoorn, C.; van Westen, B.; Cohn, N.; Strypsteen, G.; Skaden, J.; Agrawal, N. and Garcia Alvarez, M. (2023). AeoLiS. Computer software, version 3.0.1.\n\n\n\nvan Rijn, L. C. and Strypsteen, G. (2020). A fully predictive model for aeolian sand transport. Coastal Engineering 156, 103600.\n\n\n\n","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"Simon Frost\n\nOverview\nA transect\nSolving\nFetch\nBed change\nStepping\nReferences","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Overview","page":"Getting started","text":"AeoLiS models the wind-blown transport of sand over a bed whose supply of loose sediment can be limited (Hoonhout and de Vries 2016). Sand in the air relaxes towards a saturated concentration set by the wind’s shear velocity, and the sand that moves is taken from, or added to, the top layer of the bed. This vignette sets up the simplest case, a dry transect under a steady wind, and introduces the problem, solution and plotting interface.\n\nENV[\"GKSwstype\"] = \"100\"\nusing Aeolis\nusing Plots","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#A-transect","page":"Getting started","text":"A model is described by AeolisParameters, whose keywords have the names of an AeoLiS configuration file. The grid is a vector of cross-shore positions (xgrid_file) with a bed level for each (bed_file); the wind is a time series with columns time (s), speed (m/s) and direction (degrees, nautical: 270 is wind from the west, blowing towards increasing x).\n\nx = collect(0.0:1.0:150.0)\nzb = fill(1.0, length(x))\np = AeolisParameters(; xgrid_file=x, bed_file=zb,\n                     wind_file=[0.0 10.0 270.0; 86400.0 10.0 270.0],\n                     grain_size=0.0003, dt=600.0, tstop=6 * 3600.0, output_times=3600.0,\n                     output_vars=[:zb, :Ct, :Cu, :qs, :pickup, :ustar])\n\nAeolisParameters\n  time: 0.0 to 21600.0 s, dt = 600.0 s\n  scheme: euler_backward, solver: trunk\n  fractions: 1, layers: 3\n  enabled: process_wind, process_transport, process_bedupdate, process_threshold, th_grainsize\n\nUnset parameters take the AeoLiS defaults; parameter_info lists them with their units.\n\nfilter(r -> r.name in (:T, :Cb, :Aa, :k, :z, :nlayers, :layer_thickness), parameter_info())\n\n7-element Vector{NamedTuple{(:name, :type, :default, :unit, :description)}}:\n (name = :nlayers, type = Int64, default = 3, unit = \"-\", description = \"Number of bed layers\")\n (name = :layer_thickness, type = Float64, default = 0.01, unit = \"m\", description = \"Thickness of bed layers\")\n (name = :Aa, type = Float64, default = 0.085, unit = \"-\", description = \"Constant in the grain size threshold\")\n (name = :z, type = Float64, default = 10.0, unit = \"m\", description = \"Measurement height of wind velocity\")\n (name = :k, type = Float64, default = 0.001, unit = \"m\", description = \"Bed roughness\")\n (name = :Cb, type = Float64, default = 1.5, unit = \"-\", description = \"Constant in the Bagnold formulation\")\n (name = :T, type = Float64, default = 1.0, unit = \"s\", description = \"Adaptation time scale in the advection equation\")","title":"A transect"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Solving","page":"Getting started","text":"AeolisProblem wraps the parameters and a time span, and solve runs it with the scheme and transport solver named by the parameters (here the AeoLiS default, implicit Euler with the trunk solver):\n\nprob = AeolisProblem(p)\nsol = solve(prob)\n\nAeolisSolution with 7 output times from 0.0 to 21600.0 s, retcode Success\n  saved: Ct, Cu, pickup, qs, ustar, zb\n\nLike AeoLiS, output is saved at the start and whenever output_times seconds have passed. A transect is run as a grid of three identical rows; transect returns the middle row of a saved variable, with time as the last dimension.\n\nCt = transect(sol, :Ct)        # (x, fraction, time)\nsize(Ct), sol.t\n\n((151, 1, 7), [0.0, 3600.0, 7200.0, 10800.0, 14400.0, 18000.0, 21600.0])","title":"Solving"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Fetch","page":"Getting started","text":"Sand enters the air at the upwind end with no sediment in it and the concentration grows towards saturation over a distance set by the adaptation length u T: 10 m here, since by default grains move at the wind speed and T = 1 s. Downwind of a few adaptation lengths the wind carries as much sand as it can and the bed neither erodes nor accretes.\n\nCu = transect(sol, :Cu)\nplot(x, Ct[:, 1, end]; lw=2, label=\"concentration Ct\", xlabel=\"x (m)\", ylabel=\"kg/m²\")\nplot!(x, Cu[:, 1, end]; lw=2, ls=:dash, label=\"saturated concentration Cu\")\nxlims!(0, 60)\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe flux is the concentration times the grain velocity:\n\nqs = transect(sol, :qs)\nplot(x, qs[:, 1, end]; lw=2, xlabel=\"x (m)\", ylabel=\"flux (kg/m/s)\", label=\"\")\n\n(Image: )","title":"Fetch"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Bed-change","page":"Getting started","text":"The sand comes from the upwind part of the transect, which erodes; the plot recipe draws a saved variable along a transect at several output times.\n\nplot(sol, :zb; xlims=(0, 60), legend=:bottomright)\n\n(Image: )\n\ndz = transect(sol, :zb)[:, end] .- transect(sol, :zb)[:, 1]\nplot(x, 1000 .* dz; lw=2, xlabel=\"x (m)\", ylabel=\"bed level change (mm)\", label=\"\",\n     xlims=(0, 60))\n\n(Image: )\n\nThe erosion stays near the upwind boundary because downwind cells are saturated. The eroded mass is close to the mass that left the downwind end, estimated here from the hourly saved fluxes (the model steps every ten minutes, so the estimate is rough):\n\nrhob = p.rhog * (1 - p.porosity)\neroded = -sum(dz) * rhob                    # kg per metre width, cells of 1 m\noutflow = sum(qs[end, 1, 2:end]) * 3600.0   # flux at the last cell, hourly samples\n(eroded=round(eroded; digits=1), outflow=round(outflow; digits=1))\n\n(eroded = 54.7, outflow = 49.7)","title":"Bed change"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#Stepping","page":"Getting started","text":"init returns an integrator that can be advanced step by step; integ[:zb] reads a state variable and assigning to it changes the state.\n\ninteg = init(prob)\nstep!(integ, 3)\ninteg.t, maximum(integ[:Ct])\n\n(1800.0, 0.00023007615354468503)","title":"Stepping"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/01_getting_started/#References","page":"Getting started","text":"<div id=\"refs\" class=\"references csl-bib-body hanging-indent\">\n\n<div id=\"ref-Hoonhout2016\" class=\"csl-entry\">\n\nHoonhout, Bas M., and Sierd de Vries. 2016. “A Process-Based Model for Aeolian Sediment Transport and Spatiotemporal Varying Sediment Availability.” Journal of Geophysical Research: Earth Surface 121: 1555–75. https://doi.org/10.1002/2015JF003692.\n\n</div>\n\n</div>","title":"References"}]
}
